[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): The Annotator comments on the Immortal Game"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/6RDPlV0m"]
[Date "2020.03.16"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C33"]
[Opening "King's Gambit Accepted: Bishop's Gambit, Bryan Countergambit"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "The Annotator comments on the Immortal Game"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/7uiI9P9K"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.16"]
[UTCTime "20:26:54"]

{ [How not to annotate a game.]
- }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit is now an outworn, unsophisticated debut, and White should have paid the price for it even at the time. } 2... exf4 { Of course Black accepts. } 3. Bc4 Qh4+ { Black loses no time getting his attack started, even if he only attacks with just a Queen and pawn. } 4. Kf1 b5 { Either Black was hoping White would not notice his Bishop is now attacked or he read up on the Bryan Counter Gambit. } 5. Bxb5 { A true man does not turn down a gambit.
[In an exhibition game Garry Kasparov and Nigel Short picked this opening out of a hat. Kasparov, playing Black, was very upset about having to play the Black side of the Bryan Counter Gambit and did not try at all to win. See
Short, Nigel D - Kasparov, Garry, 1-0, London rapid m theme, 1993,
https://lichess.org/vzj47kQS
and
https://www.chessgames.comperl/chessgame?gid=1070668] } 5... Nf6 6. Nf3 { White hopes Black misses the attack on his Queen. } 6... Qh6 { Nope, not falling for that. } 7. d3 Nh5 { Black overprotects f4. } 8. Nh4 { White thanks Black for his Knight move which allows White to get his Knight to f5 via h4. } 8... Qg5 { Black, seeing a half-move ahead, moves his Queen now so it will not be attacked by White's next. } 9. Nf5 { Anyway. } 9... c6 { Black probably played ...d6 and White complained that the move was illegal. } 10. g4 { White would rather counter-attack than move his Bishop again. } 10... Nf6 { Black values Knights more than Bishops. } 11. Rg1 { An interesting sharp double-edged move which proves taxing for both players. } 11... cxb5 { What did I say about gambits? Right - they must be accepted. } 12. h4 { White: Got your Queen! } 12... Qg6 { Black: No, you don't. } 13. h5 { White: Yes, I do. } 13... Qg5 { Black: No, you don't! } 14. Qf3 { Of course, as White wants to play Bxf4 so he can attack Black's Queen again. } 14... Ng8 { Black wants to respond "No, you don't." one more time. Also, he sets up for the next game. } 15. Bxf4 { White: Got your Queen. } 15... Qf6 { Black: No, you don't! And stop saying that!
-
Black should have played 15...Qd8 so as to be finished with his plan to set up for the next game. } 16. Nc3 { Black's Queen has many safe places to go, so White stops attacking it. } 16... Bc5 { Black changes his mind about setting up for the next game and instead realizes that he best get another piece developed. He can then attack again with that piece and his Queen. } 17. Nd5 { White goes back to his theme - attack the Queen. } 17... Qxb2 { Black was in a quandary over which sacrifice to accept - 17...Qxb2 or 17...Bxg1. But 17...Qxb2 allows White to sacrifice more material, so Black's choice was easy. Oh, and 18.Nxf6 would have been unpleasant after 17...Bxg1. } 18. Bd6 { Of course, White is not about to defend his a1-Rook. } 18... Bxg1 { What was Black thinking?? 18...Qxa1+ was *check* and winning the sacrificed Rook. Don't overlook checks, they might be mate. } 19. e5 { White is not going to play 19.Re1 when he can instead give Black yet another try at accepting White's sacrifice. } 19... Qxa1+ { Black finally "gets it". } 20. Ke2 Na6 { Black wakes up and notices that 21.Nxg7+ Kd8 22.Bc7# is checkmate. Black gets another chance to put a Knight on the side of the board. } 21. Nxg7+ { White: Check. } 21... Kd8 22. Qf6+ { White: Checkmate. } 22... Nxf6 { Black: I don't think so. } 23. Be7# { White: Now it's checkmate.
Black: Sigh.
-
[Oh yes, this is The Immortal Game played by Adolf Anderssen and Lionel Kieseritzky June 21, 1851 London. Soltis knew his readers would know the game. You can find Soltis' copy of his newspaper column "Words Versus Moves", which inspired my annotation of the game, in his book "Karl Marx Plays Chess: And other reports on the world's oldest game", 1991.] } 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/nIoPxDql"]
[Date "2020.03.08"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C77"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Morphy Defense, Wormald Attack"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/DE72zLTB"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.08"]
[UTCTime "22:02:47"]

{ [HTIYC #I]
We begin with a game that features an opening that you have probably seen, and may even have played - the Ruy Lopez. Do some research on the Morphy Defense (3...a6) and the Wormald Attack (5.Qe2).

Play through the entire game. Pretend that you are one of the opponents - or both! Summarize the game.
-
A: The opening of this game is the Wormald Attack in the Morphy Defense of the Ruy Lopez. Black's main plan, given his moves, should have been to play on the queenside, while White's main plan was to maintain the center and play on the kingside. Black's placement of his Queen Bishop on g4 was not in accord with his played moves . I'd say he was mixing up systems, but Black probably did not know the systems. This caused Black's attack on the queenside to fail. Black compounded his difficulties by opening lines in the center. Both sides made tactical errors, but the last tactical error was by Black. White won.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 { Ruy Lopez.
Why is White's threat to win a pawn with 4.Bxc6 and 5.Nxe5 a sham threat?
-
A: Because Black recovers the lost pawn with 5...Qd4 or 5...Qg5 - both of which attack the Knight on e5 and a pawn. We covered this in the Beginner Course here:
https://lichess.org/study/IB6tbWsz } 3... a6 { Since White's threat was a sham, Black can ignore it. However, what is the point of Black's 3...a6?
-
A: It took a long time for chess theory to decide this was a good move for Black. Besides being able to play ...b5 at some point to break the pin (assuming White retreats the Bishop), Black gets his queenside action kick-started. } 4. Ba4 { What would be the pros/cons of 4.Bxc6?
-
A: The Exchange variation trades Bishop for Knight, giving Black a Bishop pair but also a doubled c-pawn. The variation has a long history. Black must play actively.
See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruy_Lopez,_Exchange_Variation } 4... Nf6 { Stockfish wanted to play 4... g6. What do you think of that move?
-
A: Looking in the OB we find it has been played before. The mainline idea is to hold the center pawn structure and play on the kingside. See the next line, which contains the top moves from the OB. } (4... g6 5. c3 d6 6. d4 Bd7 7. O-O Bg7 8. Re1 Nge7 9. d5 Nb8 10. c4 O-O { Note that Black's center pawn chain is pointing kingside. Per Sillman's rule, that is the "side" of the board on which Black should play. } 11. Nc3 h6 12. b4 f5 13. Bb2 f4 { White plays on the queenside - Black on the kingside. }) 5. Qe2 { What was Black's threat? What is White going to do if Black plays 5...b5 6.Bb3 Nd4?
-
A: Black was threatening 5...b5 6.Bb3 Nxe4. The given line for Black would be a tactical error on Black's part after 5.Qe2. See the coming line. } 5... d6 { This move is rarely played (see the OB). Why? What are its pros/cons.
-
A: The move protects the e5-pawn. However, it cuts down the mobility of the King Bishop and it pins the Queen Knight. The issue with the move is that it is too passive given that Black has better alternatives. This is why it is rarely played in the OB. } (5... b5 6. Bb3 Nd4? 7. Nxd4 c5 (7... exd4? 8. e5 $18 { The Knight is trapped. }) 8. Nf3 c4 { Noah's Ark trap, but Black has already lost a piece. } 9. Bxc4 bxc4 10. Nxe5 $18) 6. c3 { This takes away c3 from the White Queen Knight, so why is it played?
-
A: White's plan is to set up a pawn center. His Queen protects the e4-pawn, so his King Rook can come to d1 to help with playing the d4 push. The move 6.c3 also helps prepare that push while at the same time allowing the possibility of Bc2. } 6... b5 7. Bb3 { What are the pros/cons of this move versus 7.Bc2?
-
A: The original idea of the Wormald Attack was to have the Queen guard the e4-pawn, have the King Rook on d1, and have the Bishop at a possibly more active post at b3. If 7.Bb3 Na5 8.Bc2 it would seem White has lost a move, but Black has moved his Knight twice, to the side of the board, and unprotected - and White has led Black down the line where Black should play queenside. On 7.Bc2, Black can develop his King Bishop at e7, castle, and then pin at g4. Then his Queen protects his Queen Rook and since his Knight is not on a5, Black is prepared for White's a4 move. } 7... Bg4 { We are out of book here. Black has a pin on the White King Knight. What do you think of this Bishop move?
-
A: Having played through the game, you know the Bishop might have been better kept on the queenside, given that Black is going to play ...Na5. It is not that the pin is bad per se, but that mixed with ...Na5 and playing it early (before White plays d4), it is not best. } 8. a4 { What is White threatening? Why didn't White play the planned pawn push - 8.d4?
-
A: White is threatening to win a pawn since the b5-pawn is attacked twice and defended once. The move 8.d4 is premature. White's best is then to sacrifice a pawn for development. See the coming line. } { [%cal Ge2b5,Ga4b5,Ra6b5] } (8. d4 exd4 9. O-O (9. cxd4? { This is just a tactical mistake. } 9... Nxd4 $17) 9... dxc3 10. Nxc3 { White has sacrificed a pawn for development instead of the plan to hold two central pawns. }) (8. Bd5 Nxd5 9. exd5 Ne7) 8... Na5 { Stockfish wants to ignore the threat and play 8...Be7. What compensation would Black have for the loss of a pawn after 8...Be7 9.axb5 axb5 10.Rxa8 Qxa8 11.Qxb5?
-
A: See the coming variation. } { [%cal Ga5b3] } (8... Be7 9. axb5 axb5 10. Rxa8 Qxa8 11. Qxb5 { Black can now castle and swing the Rook to the b-file. White will probably retreat the Queen to e2, which gives Black the pin back. Black also will have pressure on the e4-pawn with his Queen and King Knight. Stockfish liked this better than the alternatives as it gave this a score of 0 despite Black being down a pawn. }) (8... h6 9. axb5 axb5 10. Rxa8 Qxa8 11. Qxb5 Nxe4 12. Bd5 Bd7 13. Bxe4) 9. Bc2 { The Bishop was under attack and not defended. On c2 it guards the e4 pawn and allows the King Rook to come to d1. So why does Stockfish want to play 9.Bd1? What do you think of Stockfish's suggestion?
-
A: To put this in human terms, Stockfish sees that the Bishop will need to move to d1 in some line(s) and so saves a move by moving it there now! But to a human this would be putting the Bishop out of play and on the square the King Rook wants to go. The Bishop is better on c2. } 9... Nb7 { Why is 9...Rb8 not satisfactory to hold the b-pawn? Analyze 9...Bd7.
-
A: The loose a5-Knight is the issue. See the coming lines for both of these continuations. } (9... Rb8 10. axb5 Rxb5 (10... axb5?? 11. Rxa5 $18) 11. Ba4 $18 { Pinning the Rook. }) (9... Bd7 { This is the recommended move in HTIYC. However it loses time. } 10. d4 { Threatening 11.dxe5 winning a pawn. } 10... Nc6 { Now White can take his choice between 11.Bd2, 11.0-0, or the straightforward 11.axb5. For example ... } 11. axb5 axb5 12. Rxa8 Qxa8 13. O-O (13. Qxb5?? Nxd4 14. Qd3 Nxc2+ 15. Qxc2 Qxe4+ $19) 13... Be7 $16 { White can play to win the b5-pawn by attacking it with the Knight or Bishop. }) 10. axb5 Qb8 { Instead 10...axb5 was best. Analyze that line. } (10... axb5 11. Rxa8 Qxa8 12. Qxb5+ Bd7 13. Qe2 $16 { White is a pawn up. }) 11. d4 { What do you think of this move?
-
A: White achieves his plan, but has overlooked the win of a pawn with 11.bxa6. } 11... Bxf3 { What do you think of this capture by Black?
-
A: This capture does not make sense. When you are behind in material you generally should not exchange. The pin is released. White has a good response in gxf3. Perhaps Black was hoping for Qxf3 when he could try ...axb5. You have to assume your opponent is going to play the best move. } 12. gxf3 { This gives White doubled f-pawns and a busted Kingside - so why didn't White capture with the Queen?
-
A: The Queen is guarding the b5-pawn. White is in no danger on the kingside. The f-pawn helps protect the e4-pawn. } 12... c5 { What is Black overlooking?
-
A: White can win a pawn with 13.bxc6 e.p. or 13.bxa6. } 13. Be3 { Why do you think White played this move?
-
A: White saw a double attack on d4 so brings up a second defender - missing the win of a pawn mentioned above. } 13... cxd4 { Suggested by Stockfish, but what is the issue with Black's coming exchanges of pawns?
-
A: It is usually not a good idea to open lines when you are behind in development, your King is not safe yet, and your opponent can make use of the lines. } 14. cxd4 exd4 15. Bxd4 { White has multiple threats. What are they? Besides the tactics, comment on the position.
-
A: 15.bxa6, 15.Ba4, 15.e5. Black's position is bad. He is behind in development while having to contend with White's threats. Black's Queen has very little mobility. White's doubled f-pawns and King position are moot because Black cannot take advantage of these "weaknesses". } 15... Nc5 16. bxa6 { White finally notices that he can win the a-pawn. } 16... Nfd7 { Why did Black play this and not ...Be7?
-
A: He probably did not like `17.Bxc5 followed by the check 18.Ba4+ when he'd lose the right to castle one way or the other. } 17. Nc3 { Where is this Knight going and why?
-
A: On a reasonable Black move, White can play Nd5, which is a fine outpost. Note that Black's d-pawn is isolated. } 17... Qxb2?? { A blunder. What is the refutation?
-
A: The refutation is 18.Nb5 because from there the Knight guards the d4-Bishop while the Bishop attacks the Black Queen and guards the a1-Rook. Black would lose the Queen for either the a1-Rook or could try ...Qb4 which loses the Queen to Bc3. } 18. O-O { This does protect the Queen Rook. What are White's threats now? Suggest something for Black even though his game is lost.
-
A: White is threatening 18.Nb5, 18.Rfb1 both of which win the Queen. Black could try 18...Qb8, 18...Qb6 or the counter-attack 18...Ne6. } 18... Rxa6 { Black hopes for the swindle 19.Rfb1 when he would play what? If 19.Rxa6 Nxa6 20.Qxa6 Qxc2, then what would White play? Why does Stockfish suggest 19.Rxa6?
-
A. On 19.Rfb1 Black wins with ...Rxa1, pinning the Rook. See the coming lines for the rest of the answers. } 19. Nb5 { Black resigned. What happens if he plays ...Qb4?
-
A: As mentioned before, on ...Qb4 White plays Bc3 winning the Queen. } (19. Rxa6 Nxa6 (19... Qb7 { Stockfish likes 19.Rxa6 because it thinks the score is now +12 whereas on 19.Nb5 it thinks Black should capture the a1-Rook with the Queen and end up with a score of 8.9. This is how computers think. Humans have a tendency to quit if they lose the Queen. }) 20. Qxa6 Qxc2?? { White mates in 4 moves. The pattern is worth remembering. } 21. Qc8+ Ke7 22. Nd5+ Ke6 23. Qe8+ Be7 24. Qxe7#) (19. Rfb1 Rxa1 $19) 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 2"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/ogTeN8nY"]
[Date "2020.03.09"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C30"]
[Opening "King's Gambit Declined: Classical Variation"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 2"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/5Lb60MIz"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.09"]
[UTCTime "21:51:10"]

{ [HTIYC #II]
The King's Gambit may not be familiar. The variation in this game is called the King's Gambit Declined Classical Defense 2...Bc5. Do some research on it after playing through the game. Summarize the game.
-
A: The opening is the King's Gambit Declined (KBD) Classical Defense (2...Bc5). Both sides leave their King in the Center, but White's King is more vulnerable. White fails to find a move that might have held the game (7.Bxf7+). White loses his Queen and resigns.

King Safety is the first principle of chess theory.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 { What did your research show was the point of White's gambit offer?
-
A: White offers a pawn because if it is taken White can play for d4 and then Bxf4. Even if White does not get the pawn back, after quickly developing the KN, KB, and castling, he will have a half-open f-file. White will have direct or indirect pressure on f7. } 2... Bc5 { Black has declined the gambit. What does he plan if White plays 3.fxe5? What is the main effect of the c5-Bishop?
-
A: On 3.fxe5, Black plays ...Qh4+ and White loses a Rook or gets mated. See the next line. The main effect of the c5-Bishop is that it prevents White from castling kingside and hits at d4. } 3. Nf3 { Now that ...Qh4 is stopped White threatens fxe5. Should Black play ...exf4?
-
Definitely not! White would play d4 and then Bxf4. } (3. fxe5?? Qh4+ 4. g3 (4. Ke2?? { Awful. } 4... Qxe4#) 4... Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 $19) 3... d6 { There are two attackers on e5 and only one defender. So is Black going to lose a pawn?
-
No. The reason is similar to what we saw in the Ruy Lopez. See the next line. } 4. Bc4 (4. Nxe5 dxe5) (4. fxe5 dxe5 5. Nxe5?? Qd4 { The move suggested in HTIYC. } (5... Qh4+ { This move is also mentioned in HTIYC. It is the winning move. } 6. g3 (6. Ke2?? Qxe4#) 6... Qxe4+ 7. Be2 (7. Qe2 Qxh1! { Despite the discovered check that is coming. } 8. Ng6+ { [%cal Ge2e8] } 8... Be6 9. Nxh8 Nd7 $19 { White will lose his trapped Knight for a pawn. } { [%csl Gh8][%cal Gh8g6,Gh8f7,Rg8f6,Re8c8,Ra8h8] }) 7... Qxh1+ 8. Bf1 Qxh2 $19) 6. Nd3 { HTIYC says this is the only parry. It is not, so that is a strange thing for the authors to say. } (6. Ng4 { Here is another parry. } 6... Bxg4 7. Qxg4 Qf2+ 8. Kd1) 6... Qxe4+ { HTIYC: Black has the better game.
jomega: Why?? Here is a likely continuation... } 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ 8. Bxe2 Bb6 9. b3 { Why would Black be better here? White will castle long. There is play for both sides. Stockfish gives the position a score of 0. }) 4... Nc6 { Black preferred to protect his e5-pawn rather than enter the complications of 4...Nf6. What is White's problem to solve here?
-
A: White would like to get his King out of the center - preferring to castle short. But that is currently illegal. } 5. c3 Bg4 { How does this move stop 6.d4? Or does it?
-
A: It does not stop d4 but it discourages it. See the next line. } 6. Qb3 { We are out of the book. What do you think of this move by White which seems to threaten 7.Bxf7+ and 7.Qxb7?
-
A: The problem with this unpinning and attacking move is that the tactics are iffy. White attacks but his King will not be very safe. See the variations coming off the main line. } (6. d4 exd4 { Now White has to razzle-dazzle because cxd4 fails. } 7. O-O?! (7. cxd4? Nxd4 $19) 7... dxc3+ 8. Kh1 cxb2 9. Bxb2 Nf6 10. e5 dxe5 11. Qc2 exf4 12. Bxf7+ Kxf7 13. Ng5+ Kg8 14. Qxc5 Qd5 { Putting an end to the display. } 15. Qxd5+ { There is nothing better. } 15... Nxd5 $19 { White has no attack left and is down 3 pawns. }) 6... Bxf3 7. gxf3 { What do you think of 7.Bxf7+ and then 8.gxf3?
-
A: White's position seems more risky than Black's. It is harder to find good moves for White in that line than for Black. See the next line. } (7. Bxf7+ Kf8 8. gxf3 Qh4+ 9. Kd1 { What is Black's best line? The HTIYC authors dismissed the entire line for White starting at 7.Bxf7+ because of 9...Na5. But then White has the move 10.Qe6 which actually threatens to win with 11.Bxg8 Rxg8 12.b4. White seems to hold by a thread. But Black has better with either 9...Qf2 or 9...Qh3.
-
However, the point here is that both sides are attacking with few pieces and with a compromised King position. That is fine if you have a win against the best moves your opponent can make. Otherwise it may be that your opponent has resources. } 9... Qh3 { The point being to threaten the fork at f3. } 10. Kc2 Qxf3 11. Re1 { White has several defensive tries here. } 11... Nge7 12. Bd5 Bf2 13. Rf1 exf4 { Threatening the crushing ...Ne5. } 14. Qb5 { Now 14...Ne5 is met with 15.d4. } 14... Nxd5 15. exd5 Ne7 16. Qd3 { Trading Queens to stop the attack. } 16... Qxd3+ 17. Kxd3 Bh4 18. Rxf4+ Bf6 19. c4 Ng6 $17 { Black is better because his pawn structure is better and he has the lead in Time. }) 7... Qh4+ 8. Kd1 Qh5 { The score says "Q-R4". Since Descriptive Notation is from the point of view of the player on the move, that translates as Qh5. That seems absurd. Why? If the game score is wrong, what might Black have played - assuming that he does play 9...Qxf3?
-
A: It seems absurd because 9.Bxf7+ forces 9...Qxf7 when White now has time for 10.Qxb7. Perhaps Black actually played 8...Qh3 or 8...Qxf4. Or, since this is a game between amateurs maybe Black did play 8...Qh5! } { [%csl Gb3] } 9. Qxb7?? { Threatening 10.Qxa8+ and 10.Qxc6+ ... and losing. } (9. Bxf7+ Qxf7 10. Qxb7 Kd7 11. Qxa8 Qh5 12. Re1 Qxf3+ 13. Re2 { Stockfish says the score is 0. This means it thinks someone needs to take a draw or has a draw - Black here. However ... } 13... Qh1+ 14. Re1 Qf3+ 15. Kc2 Bf2 16. Qf8 Bxe1 17. Qxg7+ Nge7 18. Qxh8 exf4 19. Qxh7 Ne5 { All of a sudden White (Stockfish) is regretting his Queen's foray. } 20. Na3 Qd3+ 21. Kb3 c5 $19 { These have all been Stockfish moves. Now it says Black's score is -8.6. }) 9... Qxf3+ { Analyze White's alternatives of 10.Be2 and 10.Ke1. } 10. Kc2 (10. Be2 Qxh1+ 11. Kc2 Qxe4+ 12. Kd1 Qh1+ 13. Kc2 Nd4+ 14. cxd4 Qxb7 $19) (10. Ke1?? Bf2+ 11. Kf1 Bg3+ 12. Kg1 Qf2#) 10... Qxe4+ { Analyze White's alternatives of 11.Kd1, 11.Bd3, and 11.Kb3. } 11. d3 (11. Kd1 Qxh1+ 12. Kc2 Nd4+ 13. cxd4 Qxb7 $19) (11. Bd3 Nd4+ 12. cxd4 Qxb7 $19) (11. Kb3 Na5+ 12. Ka4 Qxc4+ 13. b4 Nxb7 $19) 11... Nd4+ { White resigned. Why?
-
A: The check discovers an attack on the b7-Queen. } 0-1


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 3"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/uI2sp5Mb"]
[Date "2020.03.12"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C37"]
[Opening "King's Gambit Accepted: Muzio Gambit, Wild Muzio Gambit"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
