[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/O3FEVxks"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.12"]
[UTCTime "19:52:29"]

{ [HTIYC #III]
Another King's Gambit, this time White wins. Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: The opening is the Wild Muzio Gambit in the KGA. Black correctly accepts the sacrificed pieces. However, at move 8 Black needs to start looking to his King's defense. This means shielding the King, guarding key squares, and trading for the attacking pieces. When you are very far ahead in material you can give some back to stop the attack. You can even trade your Queen for adequate compensation! Black fails to find the best defense and loses.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 { [%csl Gf4] } 2... exf4 { Black accepts the gambit. } 3. Nf3 { Guarding h4. } { [%cal Gf3h4,Rd8h4,Rh4e1] } 3... g5 { HTIYC: Modern theory (1952) views this move with a very jaundiced eye. Why neglect your development and rip up your kingside pawn formation for the sake of retaining a mere pawn?
jomega: See if you can research why modern masters (as judged by the master OB on Lichess) now prefer 3...g5 - or is there better for Black?
-
A: Actually there is good evidence that 3...g5 is a draw after 4.h4 and that 3...d6 is the only winning move for Black! See
https://en.chessbase.com/post/rajlich-busting-the-king-s-gambit-this-time-for-sure } 4. Bc4 { Explain the ideas behind this move.
-
A: White wants to castle early and attack f7. } 4... g4 { After having defended his f-pawn, Black now undefends it to attack White's Knight. What do you think of this move?
-
A: Clearly Black wants to attack with ...Qh4+ after (if!) White moves the Knight. } 5. O-O { White sacrifices a Knight! Research this sacrifice. What is it called? What is the modern (2020) opinion of this sacrifice?
-
A: Of course you can tell the name of the opening because it is listed above the score! The King's Gambit Accepted, Muzio Gambit. After the Bishop sac it becomes the Wild Muzio Gambit. The modern opinion is that the sacrifice is unsound, but you had better be "booked up". } 5... gxf3 6. Bxf7+ { White sacrifices yet another piece! Stockfish gives this a score of -4.4. What is your opinion of this sacrifice?
-
A: This is very unsound. Black needs to understand how to defend such attacks though. } 6... Kxf7 7. Qxf3 { What are White's threats? What should Black's plan be? Not Black's moves, but instead a natural language description of what Black should do.
-
A: 7.Qh5+, 7.Qxf4+. Black needs to develop his pieces quickly, shield his King, and exchange the attacking pieces. Black should be willing to part with some of the material he was handed. } 7... Qf6 8. e5 { Suggest a few moves better than this.
-
A: White wants to open lines and get pieces to join the attack. Hence 8.d4, 8.Nc3, or 8.d3 were all more to the point. } (8. Qh5+ Qg6 9. Rxf4+ Nf6) 8... Qxe5 { Black is still eating material. What would have been better?
-
A: 8...Qf5. This saves the Queen while guarding h5. } (8... Qf5 { [%cal Rf3h5,Gf5h5] } 9. Qxf4 Qxf4 10. Rxf4+ Ke8 $19) 9. c3 { Comment on this move.
-
A: It is incredibly slow. White has to get his pieces into the attack quickly before Black has time to prepare. Hence still the moves indicated before d4, d3, or Nc3 were better. } (9. d4 { [%cal Gd4e5,Gc1f4] }) 9... d6 { What should Black be thinking of doing?
-
A: As we said, Black needs to get his pieces developed. Better is 9...Bh6 which develops the Bishop and protects the f6 pawn. If exchanges come there then Black will be exchanging attacking pieces. } 10. d4 Qf5 11. Bxf4 { White now has opened the f-file - an objective of the King's Gambit. } 11... Nc6 { Why was ...Nf6 better?
-
A: Clearly, White has an attack up the f-file with Black's King sitting on f7, hence ...Nf6 would shield the Black King from this frontal attack. } (11... Nf6 { [%cal Gf1f7] }) 12. Qe2 { What is White's tactical idea here? How should Black respond tactically?
-
A: Discovered attack of the f1-Rook on the Black Queen when the f4-Bishop moves. Black seriously needs to play ...Nf6. } (12. d5 Nce7) 12... Kg6 13. Bg3 { Discovering an attack on the Black Queen. } 13... Qg5 { Why would HTIYC suggest 13...Qxf1+ instead? Why does Stockfish think that is bad?
-
A: HTIYC points out that after 13...Qxf1+, Black gets adequate compensation for his Queen. They suggest practicing the resulting position with Black to learn the superiority of Rook and two minor pieces against the Queen. Stockfish thinks 13...Qe6 is winning for Black with a score -4.6, where as Stockfish gives the score after 13...Qxf1+ as +1.3. Following are some sample games from this position played against Stockfish. } (13... Qxf1+ 14. Qxf1 Nf6 { [%csl Ga8,Ga1,Gb1,Gc6,Gg3,Gc8,Rf6,Rf8,Rh8,Rf1] } 15. Nd2 Bg7 16. Bh4 Rf8 17. Qd3+ Bf5 18. Qb5 Bh6 19. Nf1 Bd7 20. Ng3 Nxd4 21. Qxb7 Be3+ 22. Kh1 (22. Kf1?? Ne4+ 23. Ke1 Bd2+ 24. Kd1 Bg4+ 25. Ne2 Bxe2#) 22... Ng4 { Black is threatening to win with 23...Nf2+. } 23. Rf1 Rxf1+ 24. Nxf1 Rf8 25. Nxe3 (25. Ng3?? Bc6 26. Qa6 Nf2+ 27. Kg1 Ne4+ 28. Kh1 Bb5 29. Qb7 Nf2+ 30. Kg1 Ne2+ 31. Kf1 Nxg3+ 32. Ke1 Nd3+ 33. Kd1 Rf1+ 34. Kc2 Bc4 35. Qe4+ Nxe4 36. Be1 Rxe1 37. b3 Rc1#) 25... Nxe3 26. Qe4+ Ndf5 27. g4 Bc6 28. Qxc6 Nxh4 29. Qe4+ Kg7 30. Qe7+ (30. Qxe3?? Rf1+ 31. Qg1 Rxg1+ $19) 30... Rf7 31. Qg5+ Kf8 32. Qd8+ Kg7 33. Qg5+ $10 { And Stockfish gets a draw. }) 14. Qe8+ { Black resigned. HTIYC is highly critical of this resignation, calling it a minor blunder. What is your opinion of Black's resignation here?
-
A: Non masters should not resign! This is not just my opinion; for example Silman says the same thing. There is much to be learned and you will not learn it by resigning. Yes, Black should lose - but make White earn it.
-
Here is a possible continuation... } 14... Kh6 (14... Kg7 15. Qxf8+ Kg6 16. Qf7+ Kh6 17. Bf4 $18) 15. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4h6,Gf1f4] } 15... Qxf4 16. Rxf4 Be7 { HTIYC: Black will lose because of the exposed position of his king; but the win still remains to be demonstrated. } 17. g4 Be6 18. Qxa8 Nd8 19. Nd2 Nf6 20. Re1 Rg8 21. h3 Kg7 22. Qb8 Nd5 23. Rf3 Bh4 24. Ref1 Nc6 25. Qxb7 Nd8 26. Qxa7 Nc6 27. Qb7 Nd8 28. Qb5 h6 29. Ne4 Ne7 30. Nf6 Bxf6 31. Rxf6 Bxa2 32. d5! Bxd5 33. c4 c6 34. Qb4 Nf7 { Black cannot afford to lose d6, but now mate is coming. } 35. cxd5 Ne5 36. Qxd6 N7g6 37. Qc7+ (37. Rxg6+ Nxg6 38. Qc7+ Ne7 39. Qxe7+ Kh8 40. Qe5+ Rg7 41. Rf7 cxd5 42. Qxg7# { This also works. }) 37... Kh8 38. Rf7 Nxf7 39. Rxf7 Rg7 40. Rxg7 cxd5 41. Rh7+ Kg8 42. Qg7# 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 4"]
