{ [HTIYC #IV]
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: The opening is Petrov's Defense. Black's attempt to immediately counter-attack (3...Nxe4) and then regain a lost pawn does not work. White should not have tried so hard to hold the won pawn because he had a much stronger attacking move (7.Nc3). The tactical crises comes and Black fails to find the best 9th move. Black would still have lost with best play, but it is important to find the best defense. A cute pinning pattern we saw in the Beginner Course wins Black's Queen and he resigns.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 { Is Black justified in counterattacking so early in the game? What do the stats in the master OB suggest about this opening?
-
A: With the master OB saying 58% of the games are draws one would conclude that the move 2...Nf6 is drawish. This is the general consensus from other sources as well. } 3. Nxe5 Nxe4 { HTIYC marks this a bad move by Black. What is wrong with it?
-
A: The opening of the e-file is dangerous for Black because White has the move! Black is not lost here, but needs to play carefully. Beginners tend to get burned as Black after this move. } 4. Qe2 Qe7 { Why doesn't Black just move his Knight back to f6 or protect it with, say, ...d5?
-
A: The first is a blunder that loses the Queen while the second loses a pawn even with best play. See the coming lines. } (4... Nf6?? { Losing the Queen to a discovered check. This is a well known opening trap and we covered it in the earlier courses. } 5. Nc6+ $18) (4... d5? 5. d3 Qe7 6. dxe4 Qxe5 7. exd5 Bd6 8. Qxe5+ Bxe5 9. Nd2 $16) 5. Qxe4 d6 { HTIYC: This will regain the piece, as White's attacked Knight is pinned and cannot retreat. But White will remain at least a pawn to the good.
jomega: Explain that last sentence.
-
A: After 6.d4, White will have two attackers on e5 so Black will lose a pawn. Black's attempt to not lose a pawn is shown in the game to not work. } 6. d4 f6 { Why do you think Black played this pawn move? What was a better move?
-
A: Black is trying to not lose a pawn. Best was 6...dxe5. } 7. f4 { Why is this move suspect? What were some better moves here for White?
-
A: White is trying to win a pawn, but the move blocks his QB and weakens squares. Instead, White had 7.Nc3 followed soon by Nd5. See the coming line. } (7. Nc3 { White has protected his Queen and his threatening to move his KN, hence Black must now capture the e5-Knight. } 7... dxe5 8. Nd5 Qf7 9. dxe5 f5 (9... fxe5?? 10. Qxe5+ Be7 (10... Be6 11. Nxc7+ $18) 11. Nxc7+ $18) 10. Qc4 $18 { White's Queen is protected by the f1-Bishop. White has a pawn and the initiative. }) 7... Nd7 { Black insist on winning the battle on e5. } 8. Nc3 { Analyze the choices 8...fxe5, and 8...f5 for Black. } 8... dxe5 (8... fxe5 9. fxe5 dxe5 10. Nd5 Nf6 11. Nxe7 Nxe4 12. Nxc8 Rxc8 13. Bd3 $16 { Black loses a pawn. }) (8... f5 9. Qe3 dxe5 10. fxe5 $18) 9. Nd5 Qd6 { HTIYC rightly says that 9...Qe6?? loses the Queen to 10.Nx7+. However, they then state that Black should have played the line 9...Qd8 10.fxe5 fxe5 11.dxe5 "White comes out a valuable pawn to the good." What do you think of that recommended line?
-
A: Their line misses that 10...fxe5 is a blunder. See the next line. } (9... Qd8 10. fxe5 fxe5?? { Black should play ...f5. } (10... f5 11. Qd3 $18) 11. Be2! { Threatening mate in 3 starting with 11.Bh5+. } (11. dxe5?? { Returning the blunder with a White blunder. Yes, White is up a pawn, but better to win outright. }) 11... g6 12. O-O { Threatening 12.Qf5!, 12. Qf3, and 12.Bb5. Black is lost. } 12... Be7 13. Bh6 { White's attack wins easily because Black's King is stuck in the center. For example... } 13... c6 14. Qf3 Nf6 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. dxe5 Qd4+ 17. Kh1 Qxe5 18. Rae1 Kd8 19. Rd1+ Bd7 20. Qh3 Qe7 21. Bg4 Re8 22. Rxd7+ Qxd7 23. Bxd7 $18) 10. fxe5 fxe5 11. dxe5 { Analyze the Black choices of 11...Qxe5, and 11...Nxe5.
-
A: See the coming lines. } 11... Qc6 (11... Qxe5 12. Nxc7+ Kd8 13. Qxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxa8 { White is up a Rook and pawn. White's Bishops and Rooks will quickly take up aggressive posts. Black can attempt to win the a8-Knight, but it does not look hopeful. For example... } 14... Bf5 15. Bg5+ Kc8 16. O-O-O Nc6 17. Bb5 Kb8 18. Bxc6 { Removing the defender of d8 and winning a piece. } 18... Bb4 (18... bxc6 19. Rd8+ $18) 19. Bd7 Bxd7 20. Rxd7 Kxa8 21. Rhd1 $18) (11... Nxe5 12. Bf4 Be6 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. Bc4 { Black counter-attack comes to an end and the pinned e5-Knight is lost. } 14... Re8 15. Bxe5 $18) 12. Bb5! { We saw this pattern in the Beginner Course here:
https://lichess.org/study/s3iOCawc/uLOMJ7cw
- } 12... Qc5 13. Be3 { Black resigned. Can Black get two pieces for his Queen by playing 13...Qxd5 14.Qxd5 c6 forking the Queen and Bishop?
-
A: No, because White would have 15.Qe6+. See the coming line.
- } 13... Qxd5 14. Qxd5 c6 15. Qe6+ Be7 16. O-O! Nf6 17. Bxc6+ bxc6 18. Qxc6+ Kf7 19. Qxa8 $18 1-0


