{ This is a Vote Chess game played by club members whose strength would average about 1100 ELO. The opponent is the commercial chess program Lv.100 playing at level 30 (1189 ELO). NB: The program never resigns!
The club played White from a position in the Sicilian Dragon. The idea was to practice a well known opening of the "mobile type" per Kotov's categories in "Think Like a Grandmaster".
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: The Sicilian Dragon was an unfamiliar opening for the White team. They correctly found the strategy to attack kingside. As a group, the team usually avoids outright tactical blunders, but still misses tactical opportunities (14.Ncb5, 14.Qg5). Yet the team won a Queen and pawn for a Rook and showed that they understood how to covert to a won endgame. They need to learn though that one Queen against a lone King is sufficient to mate and more likely to avoid stalemate for this level player.
- }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O { -
The game was started from this position.
- } 8. Bc4 b6 { Not in the OB. What is your opinion of this move and the plan it implies?
-
A: The move weakens the light squares. The fianchettoed QB would not work well here. } 9. Qd2 { The standard battery against the fianchettoed Dragon Bishop. } 9... e5 { Not only is this tactically incorrect but it does not fit in with Black's best plans. Explain.
-
A: 9...e5 creates a backward d-pawn that is immediately lost. Black's best plan in this opening is to play on the queenside. His KB is on the diagonal leading to a1 and should not be blocked by the pawn at e5. Black should have been looking to play a3, b5 with his pawns and control of the c-file with a Rook at c8 etc. } 10. Ndb5 h5 { What is the point of this move?
-
A: It is pointless and weakens g6. } 11. Nxd6 Bd7 { Suggest a better move for Black.
-
A: 11...Be6 would blunt the Bishop on c4, which is pinning the f7-pawn. } 12. O-O-O { Why didn't White castle kingside? What do you think of trading the c4-Bishop and d6-Knight for the pawn and Rook with 12.Bxf7+.
-
A: White wants to leave open the possibility of a kingside pawn storm. The suggested trade is a really bad idea. In general it is a bad idea to trade two pieces for a Rook and pawn, but here it is even sillier as White would be trading two of his very active pieces for a Rook that is very limited in mobility. } 12... Nc6 13. Bh6 { What is the strategic idea behind White's move?
-
A: Remove the defender of the dark squares on the kingside. } 13... Qc7 { Why is this bad? Suggest better moves for Black.
-
A: This puts the Queen on a square that is easily attacked and with advantage to White. Black had to do something about the pinned f7-pawn. Black should play 13...Qe7 or 13...Kh7 or 13...Be6. } 14. Bxg7 { White was intent on the plan to weaken the dark squares on the kingside. There are 3 better tactical moves. What are they?
-
A: 14.Ncb5 and then 15.Qg5, or 14.Qg5 immediately, or even 14.Nxf7 Rxf7 15.Nb5 Qb8 16.Nd6 and then Qg5. } 14... Kxg7 15. Qg5 { What is White's threat?
-
A: White threatens 15.Qxf6+! Kxf6 16.Nd5+ which wins the Black Queen via a fork and hence wins a piece. White even wins more material soon afterward. } 15... Qd8 { A blunder. What was a better defense?
-
A. Instead ...Be6. } 16. Nf5+ { Black is pinned to death. } 16... Bxf5 { Show that other moves get Black mated. } (16... Kg8 17. Qxg6+ Kh8 18. Qg7#) (16... Kh8 17. Qh6+ Nh7 (17... Kg8 18. Qg7#) 18. Qg7#) (16... Kh7 17. Qh6+ Kg8 18. Qg7#) 17. Rxd8 Raxd8 18. exf5 { White has a won game with a Queen and pawn for a Rook. But at this level players can have a hard time winning a won game, so we continue. } 18... Nh7 19. Qg3 Na5 20. Qxe5+ Nf6 21. Ne4 { Pile on the pinned piece. } 21... Nxc4 22. Qxf6+ Kh6 23. Rd1 { What is White's plan? Why is it better than avoiding exchanges with Stockfish's Ng5?
-
A: White wants to get rid of one of Black's Rooks. This will make it easier for the Queen and Knight to mop up. White will then try to win a few pawns, trade the Knight for the Black Knight and then (horrors!) trade his Queen for the Black Rook to enter a simply won King and pawn endgame. } 23... Rxd1+ 24. Kxd1 Ne3+ 25. Kc1 Nxf5 26. Ng3 { What is White' s threat?
-
A. Nxf5 winning a Knight because of the pin. } 26... Nxg3 27. hxg3 Kh7 28. Qe7 { White wins another pawn. } 28... Ra8 29. Qxf7+ Kh6 30. Qb7 { White wins another pawn. } 30... Re8 31. Qxa7 Re6 32. Kd1 Rd6+ 33. Ke2 Rc6 34. Qa4 Re6+ 35. Kf2 g5 36. c4 { With a qualitative pawn majority on the queenside and no kingside issues, what should be White's strategy here?
-
A: As mentioned before, trade Queen for Rook and win the King and pawn endgame. } 36... Kg7 37. Qd7+ Kf6 38. Qxe6+ { What do you think of this move? Stockfish does not approve. Why?
-
A: This is the simplest win. Stockfish only counts points. } 38... Kxe6 39. Ke3 Kf7 40. b4 Ke6 41. a4 { Winning this is child's play. Just don't stalemate. :-) } 41... Kd6 42. a5 bxa5 43. bxa5 Kc5 44. Ke4 { As we covered in the Intermediate Course, Black cannot take the c-pawn because he then steps out of the "square of the pawn" of the a-pawn. In other words, the a-pawn would queen. } 44... h4 45. gxh4 gxh4 46. f4 Kc6 47. Ke5 Kc5 48. Ke6 Kc6 49. f5 Kc5 50. f6 Kxc4 51. f7 h3 52. gxh3 Kb4 53. a6 Kc3 54. a7 Kb2 55. a8=Q Kb3 56. f8=Q { The team insisted on getting two Queens. This is why some of them end up stalemating in such positions. } 56... Kb2 57. Qff3 Kb1 58. Qg2 Kc1 59. Qa1# 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 6"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/oZQjQEdx"]
[Date "2018.11.29"]
[White "HS Club Members"]
[Black "Lv.100 Level 50"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[BlackElo "1614"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B22"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Delayed Alapin Variation"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/Z76dYrws"]

{ This is a Vote Chess game played by club members whose strength would average about 1100 ELO. The opponent is the commercial chess program Lv.100 playing at level 50 (1614 ELO). NB: The program never resigns! The club is playing White.

Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: The team first balked at playing level 50, which advertises that it is 1614 ELO. They should take Silman's advice and not look at their opponent's rating!
The opening was the Sicilian Defense: Delayed Alapin Variation. The White team still plays the opening by rules of thumb. They should start the transition to Silman's imbalances theory soon. Despite this, White played the opening well and had the initiative. At move 16 White failed to find the strongest line. Learning pawn structures and the plans for both sides would help that. The team likes to trade pieces instead of maintaining tension - another lesson to learn. At the crucial moment (White's 19) it was necessary to find the best move. White's 24th move was excellent as was White's 32. It was unfortunate that White failed to find a win, but did get a draw.
- }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. c3 d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. d4 { Comment on 5.Bb5+ instead.
-
A: Black's simple response would be ...Bd7. Why would White want to trade his good Bishop, for Black's bad Bishop? } 5... Nf6 6. Be2 { The team consider 6.Na3. This puts the Knight on the rim (dim), so why would White do that?
-
A: The idea would be play Nc2. The Knight would support the center and not block the QB. } 6... Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. Bf4 { White wanted to develop the QB. What are some other plans for White?
-
A: 8.c4 and then 9.dxc5 would avoid the isolani. 8.Na3 and then Nc2 as mentioned before. 8.Be3 would also be possible. White has to decide whether to have the isolani or not. } 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 { Comment on the pawn structure.
-
A: White has an isolated d-pawn; an isolani. We covered this in the previous courses. } 9... Qe4 10. Qd2 Nd5 11. Bg3 O-O 12. Nc3 { Development with tempo. } 12... Nxc3 { Comment on this exchange.
-
A: This seems to help White because after the pawn recapture White will no longer have an isolated d-pawn. } 13. bxc3 { What kind of pawn feature is the c and d-pawns? Contrast the minor pieces for both sides.
-
A: Silman calls them "hanging squares" referring to the holes at c4 and c5. If Black occupies those squares, White can be in trouble. White has the superior minor pieces. Black's QB is bad and his QN has little mobility. } 13... Rd8 14. Bd3 Qg4 15. Qc2 { Deliberately trying to get Black to move a kingside pawn. } 15... Qh5 { Not at level-50 it seems. } 16. Rab1 { Grabbing an open file. Was 16.Ne5 better?
-
A: 16.Ne5 was strong. See the next line. } (16. Ne5 { Silman's suggestion in such positions is that White should try to make use of e5 for an attack. If nothing else, this keeps Black from immediately implementing his aforementioned plan of occupation of c4 and d5. } 16... Bd7 (16... Nxe5 17. Bxe5 $16 { Now Black has to decide what to do about White's aggressively developed pieces. }) 17. Rfe1 $16) 16... Bd6 { Why is this questionable?
-
A: Black should play ...b6 so as to get his QB developed and blunt the b1-Rook. } 17. Bxd6 { The team needs to learn the idea of maintaining tension. Beginner and Intermediate players like to trade. } 17... Rxd6 18. Rfe1 Rd7 { Black has been missing an obvious move for himself. What is it? What should White play next? Comment on the positional elements that are present.
-
A: As mentioned ...b6. White now has 19.Rb5. As for the positional elements - White has the hanging squares, but Black cannot make use of them. Black's Bishop is bad; indeed terrible. Black's QR is locked out of the game. Black's Queen is exposed and his King is looking iffy. White has the half-open b-file and e-file. } 19. Ne5 { Given those positional elements, what should have been White's plan, and what move would have been good here?
-
A: White has so many positional pluses that he should have taken the time to look for a winning move! The time was ripe. 19.Rb5 was that move. See the next line. } (19. Rb5 Qh6 20. d5 { The keep idea. } 20... Nd8 (20... exd5?? 21. Re8#) (20... Rxd5 21. Rxd5 exd5?? 22. Re8#) 21. Ne5 Rxd5 (21... Rc7 22. Qa4 $18 { The threat is Rc5! because the Queen is then threatening Qe8#. }) 22. Rxd5 exd5 23. Qa4 { Black's back rank is weak. } 23... b5 24. Qxb5 g6 25. Nxg6! { Tactics based on the unprotected a8-Rook and d8-Knight. } 25... hxg6 26. Qxd5 Rb8 27. Qxd8+ Kg7 28. g3 { Black cannot move his Rook or Bishop. } 28... Rb6 { The best Stockfish can find. } 29. Qxc8 Qd2 30. Re3 Rf6 31. Qc5 Qxf2+ 32. Kh1 Rb6 33. Qd4+ Kh7 34. Qh4+ Kg7 (34... Kg8 35. Re8+ Kg7 36. Qh8#) 35. Qd4+ Kh7 36. Qf4 $18) 19... Nxe5 20. Rxe5 Qh6 21. Rbe1 b6 22. R1e3 { What is White's threat?
-
A. White is threatening 22.Rh3 with the attack on h7. } 22... g6 23. Rh3 { It is hard to get people to break the habit of single attacks that are easily handled. What move should White have made instead?
-
A: 23.d5 or 23. Be4 or 23. Bb5. } 23... Qg7 24. Reh5! { With Black still having an undeveloped QR and QB, White decided to exchange both his Rooks for the Black Queen. } 24... gxh5 25. Rg3 h6 { Pointless. What was better?
-
A: ...Bb7 was better. } 26. Rxg7+ Kxg7 27. Qe2 Bb7 28. Qe5+ Kg8 29. Qxh5 Rc8 { Black finally gets to attack the hanging pawns. } 30. Qxh6 Rxc3 31. Bh7+ Kh8 32. d5 { What is the point of this pawn push?
-
A: It opens the diagonal a1-h8 so when White's Queen reaches h8 it will be attacking the undefended (currently) c3-Rook. } 32... Rdc7 { Protecting his unguarded c3-Rook, but it is a blunder. Why?
-
A: White can win. See the coming line. } 33. Bg6+ { After playing so many excellent moves, White could not find the win and decided to take a draw. Give the winning line. } (33. Bc2+ { 33.Bd3 also works as the Bishop can interpose at f1 if necessary. } 33... Kg8 34. Qh7+ Kf8 35. d6! { The Bishop blocks the Black mate on c1 while White threatens mate on h8. Black loses a Rook. } 35... Ke8 36. Qg8+ Kd7 37. dxc7 Kxc7 38. Qxf7+ Kb8 39. Qf4+ Ka8 40. Be4 $18) 33... Kg8 34. Bh7+ Kh8 35. Bg6+ Kg8 36. Bh7+ Kh8 37. Bg6+ { Draw by repetition. } 1/2-1/2


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 7"]
