{ [HTIYC #V]
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: Black succumbs to an attack on f7 in a Ruy Lopez: Advanced Variation opening. Black could have avoided the worst by playing 7...Nd4 to remove the White Bishop. Instead, Black's King ends up in the center of the board trying to hold the piece he won from White's sacrifice. White played an energetic attacking game and won.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 { We've seen this now many times. } 4... b5 { Black figures to get this move in right away. What is the issue with that?
-
A: Black is driving the Bishop to b3 and from there the Bishop is attacking f7. This will be before Black has started to develop his kingside pieces. Hence, Black will be a move behind in getting castled. } 5. Bb3 Nf6 { Does this remind you of another opening? Which one? Suppose Black had played 5...Na5 6.0-0 Nxb3. Why would Black do that?
-
A: This is the same idea as the Two Knights Defense - Black attacks the e4-pawn. The idea behind the line given with 5...Na5 would be to remove White's Bishop and so relieve the attack on f7. } 6. Ng5 { White attacks with two pieces. } 6... d5 { The general solution to such an attack - counter with ...d5. } 7. exd5 Nxd5 { Black walks into the attack. What would have been better?
-
A: With 7...Nd4 Black could have captured the b3-Bishop. This move would also prevent Qf3. } 8. Nxf7 Kxf7 9. Qf3+ { White double attacks the d5-Knight with this check. Is Black forced to play ...Ke6? Analyze 9...Kg8 (yes, people have play that) and 9...Ke7.
-
A: Black will have no compensation if he does not play ...Ke6. The move 9...Kg8 gets Black mated. For both these lines see the variations. } 9... Ke6 (9... Kg8?? 10. Qxd5+ Qxd5 11. Bxd5+ Be6 12. Bxe6#) (9... Ke7? 10. Bxd5 { Winning back his piece and threatening Bxc6. } 10... Nd4 11. Qf7+ Kd6 12. Nc3 Qe7 { Trying to stop the attack by exchanging Queens. } 13. Qxe7+ Bxe7 14. Bxa8 Nxc2+ 15. Kd1 Nxa1 16. Bf3 Bf5 { Hoping to get his trapped Knight out. } 17. Be4 $18) (9... Qf6?? 10. Bxd5+ Ke7 11. Bxc6 $18) 10. Nc3 { Triple attack on the pinned d5-Knight. } 10... Ne7 { Examine instead 10...Nb4 and 10..Nd4 as alternatives.
-
A: White wins on either of those moves. } (10... Nd4 11. Bxd5+ Kd6 12. Qd1 { Simplest. } 12... c6 13. Be4 Qh4 { Threatening ...Bg4. } 14. O-O Kc7 { Not feeling safe with the King in the center. } 15. d3 Bg4 16. f3 Bc5 { Black has the initiative, but it is about to stop. } 17. Kh1 Be6 18. Be3 $18 { The Black attack is over. White is a pawn up. White can pry open the f-file after preparation. }) (10... Nb4 11. a3 c5 12. d3! c4 13. dxc4 bxc4 14. Bxc4 Nxc2+ 15. Kf1 Nxa1 16. Bxd5+ Kd7 17. Bxa8 { Now 18.Qc6 would be deadly. } 17... Kc7 18. Be3 { White has threats of either Ba7 or Nd5+. } 18... Kb8 19. Qe4 Nc2 20. Qxc2 Kxa8 21. Ke2 $18) 11. d4 { White wants more open lines. } 11... c6 { Explain why ...exd4 is bad on general terms and then specifically analyze it here.
-
A: In general, you do not open lines for your opponent's attack! } (11... exd4 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Qe4+ Kf6 14. Bxd5 $18) 12. Bg5 { A pin on the e7-Knight. } 12... Qd6? { Is Black looking at what his opponent will play next? What do you think of this move? What would be better?
-
A: Black should look at the position and see that White can play his Knight to e4, hence Black's Queen should not move to d6 because it would have to move again - Black loses Time.
Better would be 12...Bb7. See the next line. } (12... Bb7 13. O-O-O Kd7 14. dxe5 Kc8 $18 { Despite being a piece down, White is winning. Here is an example finish... } 15. Rhe1 Kb8 16. e6 Ra7 17. Qf7 b4 18. Qf4+ Qc7 19. Nxd5 cxd5 20. Qxb4 Nc6 21. Qg4 h5 22. Qf3 d4 23. Qf7 Qxf7 24. exf7 Kc7 25. Bf4+ Kb6 26. Re8 Ra8 27. Rde1 Bb4 28. R1e6 Rf8 { Black has finally unraveled his tied up position, but ... } 29. Rxa8 Bxa8 30. Rg6 Bb7 31. Rxg7 Ne7 32. a3 Bc5 33. Bh6 Bd6 34. g3 Bd5 35. Bxd5 Nxd5 36. Rg6 { Winning back the piece. } 36... Rxf7 37. Rxd6+ Kc5 38. Rxa6 Rxf2 39. b4+ Kb5 40. Ra5+ Kc6 (40... Kc4?? 41. Rc5#) 41. Bg7 Rxh2 42. Bxd4 Nc7 43. c4 $18) 13. O-O-O { This is a good move. Give another good move for White here.
-
A: Ne4 would bring the Knight into the attack. There is a hole on c5. } 13... h6 { Black is already lost. Analyze what happens if he tries 13...Kd7 to flee with the King. } (13... Kd7 14. dxe5 Qg6 15. Bxe7 Bxe7 16. Nxd5 cxd5 17. Qxd5+ $18 { Winning a Rook and the attack continues. }) 14. Ne4 { This wins, but what was the crushing move here?
-
A: Instead 14.dxe5 brings the d1-Rook alive. See the next line. } (14. dxe5 Qc5 15. Be3 Qb4 16. Nxd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5+ cxd5 18. Qxd5+ Ke7 19. Bc5+ $18 { Winning the Queen. }) 14... Qc7 15. Nc5+ Kd6 16. dxe5+ { Winning. What was the quicker mate?
-
A: 16.Qe4 mate in 3 more moves. } (16. Qe4 { Threatening Qxe5#. } 16... Ng6 17. dxe5+ Nxe5 (17... Kxc5 18. Qd4#) 18. Rxd5+ cxd5 19. Qxd5#) 16... Kxc5 { What if Black plays 16...Kxe5?
-
A: He gets mated. } (16... Kxe5 17. Qe4+ Kd6 18. Bf4+ Kxc5 19. Qd4#) 17. Be3+ Kb4 { Analyze 17...Nxe3.
-
A: Another quick mate. } (17... Nxe3 18. Qxe3+ Kb4 19. Qc3#) 18. Bxd5 Nxd5 { Is 18...cxd5 any better?
-
A: It prolongs the mate by one move. } (18... cxd5 19. Bb6 Qxc2+ 20. Kxc2 Bf5+ 21. Kd2 Bd3 { These are all spite moves. } 22. Qxd3 Rc8 23. Qb3#) 19. Bb6 { What theme is this? Analyze all the Black alternatives.
-
A: The theme is Clearance. The Bishop moves so the White Queen can mate or command the 3rd rank. White is now threatening 19.Qe4#, 19.Qb3#, and 19.Rd4#. } 19... Qxb6 (19... Nxb6 20. Qa3+ Kc4 21. Qc3#) (19... Ne3 20. Rd4+ Nc4 21. Qa3#) (19... c5 20. Qb3#) (19... Nc3 20. Rd4#) 20. Qa3+ Kc4 21. b3+ Kc3 22. Rd3# 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 8"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/sEhdogzW"]
[Date "2020.03.14"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B32"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Accelerated Dragon"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 8"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/mWPKMZ1W"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.14"]
[UTCTime "20:49:41"]

{ [TAM Test #1]
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: The opening was the Sicilian Defense: Accelerated Dragon. White played the questionable 5.Bc4 instead of the the Maroczy Bind 5.c4. Soon after, White lost three pieces for Black's Queen. This is a material advantage for Black! Black's plan should have been to stop White from harnessing the Black Knight. Then Black could develop his pieces *in cooperation fashion* and then create one or more targets. Because Black would have more pieces to attack such a target than White has for the defense, Black would win. Instead, Black gets his King Knight kicked about as White plays energetically. Black should review his thinking process at moves 20 and 22 because these are bad moves. White won.
- }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 { This is the Sicilian Defense: Accelerated Dragon. Research this opening. What are the pros/cons of this opening versus the Dragon 4...Nf6?
-
A: Black avoids the main lines of the Dragon, such as the Yugoslav Attack. However, White can play 5.c4 - the Maróczy Bind). } 5. Bc4 { Instead 5.c4 is the Maroczy Bind. Research and answer why this is called a "Bind".
-
A: The "Bind" refers to the difficulty Black would have in getting in the so called freeing move ...d5. } 5... Qa5+ 6. Bd2 { Attacking the Queen - which will move, of course. What were better moves for White?
-
A: 6.Nc3 and 6.c3 were both better moves. White's Bishop and Knight on the 4th rank are hanging. } 6... Qc5 { Analyze 7.Nxc6 and 7.Nb5. } 7. Bc3 (7. Nxc6 Qxc4 8. Bc3 Qxe4+ 9. Kf1 Qxc6 10. Bxh8 f6) 7... Qxc4 { Analyze 8.Na3. } 8. Nd2 { Since you've played through the game, you know that Black now grabs three minor pieces for his Queen. Is Black then ahead, behind, or equal in material?
-
A: Black is ahead in material. While the point system may say 9 points each, 3 minor pieces almost always beat a Queen. The reasons are that Black would eventually have more attackers than White, and that the Queen cannot be traded for a Rook or minor piece(s). } 8... Qxd4 9. Bxd4 Nxd4 10. c3 Nc6 11. O-O Bg7 12. f4 { At this point Silman asks "What is Black's correct plan?". Answer that, and also say what is White's correct plan.
-
A: This was answered in the summary of the game. White's plan is to stay as energetic as possible, keep Black's pieces from coordinating, and try to make targets. } 12... Nf6 { On Wikipedia it says "...Black avoids playing ...d7-d6, so that they can later play ...d7-d5 in one move." Should Black have played 12...d6 here? What about 12...d5?
-
A: That quote is deliberately taken out of context! The context was the beginning of the game - not now. 12...d5 is a blunder that loses material to 13.exd5. However, 12...d6 would have been an excellent move. It would stop 13.e5 and allow Black to get his pieces developed. } 13. e5 Nd5 14. Nc4 { Why is ...Nc7 better?
-
A: On c7 the Knight would not be subjected to attack by a possible a4/a5. } 14... Nb6? 15. Ne3 O-O 16. a4 { What is White's threat?
-
A: Simply 17.a5 trapping the Knight. } 16... a5 { Silman gives this move a "?". Stockfish likes it best. Why the difference?
-
A: Silman knows that Black made this move in reactionary fashion. He says it leaves holes at b5 and b6. Silman suggest the next line 16...Rb8. Silman is making a point about a detrimental attitude that Black took. Stockfish is calculating without regard to emotion. } (16... Rb8 17. a5 { Silman's move is to make a point - that Black will eventually get his pieces coordinated on this White attack. } (17. b4! { Stockfish's move for White. Now both Black Knights are in trouble. } 17... d5! { Now the harassed Knight can jump to c4 so ... } 18. exd6 exd6 19. a5 Nd7 20. b5 Ne7 21. Qxd6 Nf5 22. Nxf5 gxf5 23. Rfd1 { It is instructive to see how Stockfish ties Black's pieces up. } 23... Ra8 (23... Bxc3? 24. Ra3 Bg7 25. Qc7 Ra8 26. Rxd7 Bxd7 27. Qxd7 $18) 24. Qc7 Nf6 25. Rd8 Be6 26. Rxa8 Rxa8 27. Qxb7 Bd5 28. Qc7 Be4 { Stockfish likes White at +3. }) 17... Na8 18. Nd5 d6 19. exd6 exd6 { Silman: ...when Black's pieces will finally be able to escape from their tomb. }) 17. Qb3 Ra6 { Forced to keep from losing the Knight. } 18. Rad1 Rd8 19. Qa3 { Why did White move the Queen to a3? What should Black play?
-
A: White's move keeps Black from moving the c6-Knight while preparing for the coming 19...d6. Black should play 19...Bf8 and gets his Bishop to c5 after 20...e6. } 19... d6 20. exd6 Rxd6? { Why is this a serious error?
-
A: Because Black could have played 20...exd6 and on 21.Rxd6 played 21...Bf8 22.Rxd8 Nxd8 23.Qb3 Bc5 with the initiative. } 21. Rxd6 exd6 22. Qxd6 { White just won a pawn, but what is the real problem with Black's game?
-
A: Black's pieces are uncoordinated. } 22... Nxa4? { Black gets that pawn back. Is this Black's best move?
-
A: No. Again Black could seize the initiative with 22.Bf8 23.Qc7 Bc5. } 23. Qc7 { Comment on White's play for the last several moves.
-
A: White has the initiative and continues to try to keep Black's pieces uncoordinated. } 23... Ra8 24. Nc4 { What are White's threats?
-
A: White has 24.Nd6, 24.f5, 24.Re1. The initiative keeps Black in reactionary mode. } 24... Bf8 { Examine 24...Nc5. } (24... Nc5 25. Nd6 Bd7 26. Rd1 Bf8 27. Rd5 Bxd6 28. Qxd6 { Black loses material. For example ... } 28... b6? 29. Rxc5 bxc5 30. Qxd7 $18) 25. f5! { Why didn't White play 25.Ra1 immediately?
-
A: As shown in the next line, the Queen could get trapped. Now the Queen has an escape route. } (25. Ra1 Nc5 26. Nb6? Ne6 $19 { Trapping the Queen. }) 25... gxf5 26. Ra1 { Can Black save his Knight with 26...Nc5?
-
A: No. See the next line. } 26... Bc5+ (26... Nc5 27. Nb6 Ne6?? (27... Rb8 28. Nxc8 $18) 28. Qg3+ $18) 27. Kh1 Nxc3 { TAM: This horse, tormented throughout this game, is driven to suicide. } 28. bxc3 { Black resigned.
Is resignation appropriate here?
-
A: No. At this level one should play on. Silman makes this point in the next test. } 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 9"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/o59gT8ZH"]
[Date "2020.03.15"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C50"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo, Normal"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 9"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/Wu8l90rU"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.15"]
[UTCTime "19:58:21"]

{ [HTIYC #VI]
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: In a Giuoco Pianissimo opening, White makes a number of mistakes that allows Black to get a classic kingside attack - open a file, double Rooks, etc.
Black wins.
- }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 { The Giuoco Pianissimo. I'm sure you've seen this before. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O { Why not 5.Ng5 with a double attack on f7?
-
A: Because Black would just castle and White would not want to trade two pieces for a Rook and pawn. Hence the move would waste time. } 5... d6 6. Bg5 { Discuss the merits of this move.
-
A: It pins the f6-Knight - for a few moves. Because the pin cannot be maintained and the Bishop gets pushed to g3, where it can be attacked again and has little scope, it would be better to either play the Bishop to e3 or wait to develop the Bishop. The top move in the OB is c3, which prepares for a later d4.
This opening is all about who is going to control the center. At one point in history it was thought that 6.Be3 was an error because after 6...Bxe3 7.fxe3, White has doubled e-pawns. However his pawn on e3 would control f4 and d4 and White would have a half-open f-file. } (6. c3 a6 7. Bb3 Ba7 8. Nbd2 h6 9. Re1 g5 10. d4) 6... h6 7. Bh4 Bg4 { Analyze the move 7...g5. } (7... g5 8. Bg3 h5 { This is similar to the game except Black's Queen Bishop is not yet pinning the f3-Knight. Suppose White make the mistake of capturing on g5... } 9. Nxg5 h4 10. Nxf7 Qe7 11. Nxh8 hxg3 12. Bf7+ Kd8 13. hxg3 Qf8 { White has to give back material. } 14. Ng6 Qxf7 15. Nh4 Bg4 16. Qd2 Kd7 { Black has a strong attack. } { Bets, A. - Molchanov, Alexe, 0-1, January Lviv Tradition IM, 2018, https://lichess.org/9iHVLmqZ }) 8. c3 { Explain why White probably felt this was better than the immediate 8.h3.
-
A: Probably because he saw that Black's Queen Knight might get on d4. } (8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nd4 10. Qd1 g5 11. Bg3 b5 12. Bb3 { Black has the initiative. }) 8... g5 { Burying White's Bishop. } 9. Bg3 h5 { HTIYC gives this a "!". Stockfish says White is +0.9. Explain this.
-
A: HTIYC gives this a "!" because opening a file on the kingside is the correct plan. Stockfish finds 10.h4 as a way to stop 10...h5. See the next line for Stockfish's analysis. } 10. h3 { Explain why this move is an error.
-
A: This makes a target of the h3 pawn. You should view h3 as suspect for White if Black has not castled in the Giuoco. HTIYC points out that as long as the h, g, and f pawns stay put on the second rank, it is difficult for Black to open one of those files. } (10. h4 Nd7 11. Nbd2 gxh4 12. Bxh4 Qxh4 13. Nxh4 Bxd1 14. Raxd1 Rg8 15. Ndf3 { And here Stockfish says +1.1. }) 10... h4 11. Bh2 Bh5 { What is Black's threat? What are the plans for both sides?
-
A: Black is threatening ...g4 which will force open a file. Black's plan is to attack kingside by forcing open a file. He will castle queenside and bring over his Rooks. How the attack will be done depends on how White defends.
White's plan is to find some sort of defense. The recommended approach in many books is to counter in the center. We'll look at that shortly. } 12. Qb3 { White runs away from the pin. But this counter-attack on f7 and the queenside does not work, as we will see. } (12. d4? { This is what White would want to play - the counter-attack in the center. But it loses material because of the pinned Knight. } 12... exd4 13. cxd4 Nxd4 14. Nbd2 g4 15. Nxd4 gxh3 16. N2f3 Qd7 $19) (12. Bb5! { Now White is threatening d4. } 12... g4 13. hxg4 Bxg4 14. d4 Bb6 15. d5 { Now Black has to decide whether to continue with the kingside attack immediately or respond to the central attack. The point is that White has to play in the center to counter the Black kingside action. }) 12... g4 13. Ng5 { White is threatening Bxf7+. Play 13...g3 and calculate the best moves for both sides from there. } 13... Qd7 (13... g3?? { This is a blunder, but the point of it is to just see how White's attack would proceed. } 14. Bxf7+ Ke7 (14... Bxf7?? 15. Qxf7#) (14... Kd7?? 15. Qe6#) 15. Qe6+ Kf8 16. Bxh5 Qe7 17. Bf3 gxh2+ 18. Kxh2 Qxe6 19. Nxe6+ Kf7 20. Nxc7 Rad8 21. Nd2 $18) 14. hxg4 Nxg4 { Is Black prepared if White plays 15.Qxb7? Calculate some variations from that.
-
A: Black is prepared. See the next variation. } 15. Nh3 { Hoping to guard his King while getting his lose Knight guarded. } (15. Qxb7?? Rb8 16. Qa6 Rb6 17. Qa4 Nxh2! (17... Rg8 { -HTIYC }) 18. Kxh2 Rg8! 19. d4 { Trying to counter in the center. } 19... Rxg5 20. dxc5 Rxg2+!! 21. Kxg2 Qg4+ 22. Kh2 Qf4+ 23. Kg1 h3 24. Rd1 h2+ 25. Kg2 Bf3+ 26. Kh3 Qg4+ 27. Kxh2 Qg2#) 15... Rg8 16. Kh1 { Why did White move his King into the corner?
-
A: To get out of the pin on the f-pawn and to get his King off the file where Black's g8-Rook is. } 16... Nxh2 { What do you think of this move?
-
A: Perhaps premature. Black should castle first. However, Black is winning. } 17. Kxh2 Qg4 { Threatening ...Qxg2#. } 18. Rg1 O-O-O { Why is Black not concerned about 19.Bxf7?
-
A: See the next variation. Basically, if White captures on f7 then Black will recapture with the Bishop and bring his QR to the now open f-file. That will put 2 attackers on f2. It is a bad idea to open lines for your opponent's attack. } 19. Qc2 { Why did White move the Queen to protect f2 when he already had the h3-Knight protecting it?
-
A: Black was threatening ...Bxf2. That removes the guard on g3 allowing ...Qg3+ and then ...Bf3! Once the Knight moves, White only has the King guarding the g1-Rook while Black has two attackers on the g-file. Hence the g-pawn would be pinned. } (19. Bxf7 Bxf7 20. Qxf7 Rdf8 21. Qb3 Bxf2 $19) 19... Rg7 { Looking ahead in the score, we see that Black is going to double Rooks on the g-file. Does White have a defense?
-
A: No. Black wins as we will see. } 20. Na3 { Examine 20.Nd2. } (20. Nd2 { This blocks the White Queen's defense of f2. Let's look at ...Bxf2 and see how the attack would go. } 20... Bxf2 21. Nxf2 Qg3+ 22. Kh1 Qxf2 (22... Bf3?? { This no longer works because the a1-Rook is guarding the g1-Rook. } 23. gxf3 $18) 23. Raf1 Qg3 24. Nf3 h3 25. Nh2 Rdg8 { Threatening 26...Qxg2+ and 26....hxg2+ - both win. } 26. Rf2 { White has set up a second rank defense. } 26... Ne7 27. Qd2 Kb8 28. Qc2 { White can do nothing of value. } 28... d5 29. Bb3 hxg2+ 30. Rgxg2 (30. Rfxg2 { Allows mate in 4. } 30... Bf3 31. Nxf3 Qxf3 { Now the h-file is open and Black threatens ...Rh8#. } 32. Rd1 Rxg2 33. Qxg2 Qxg2#) 30... Qh4 31. Rxg7 Rxg7 32. Bxd5 Rg3 { Threatening ...Bf3+. } 33. Bc4 Bf3+ 34. Rxf3 Rxf3 { Threatening ...Qe1+ with mate to come. } 35. Qd1 Rf2 36. Qg1 Rxb2 37. Bxf7 Re2 { ...Re1 would pin the Queen. The Knight is already pinned. White's Queen has to stay were she is to guard against the mate threat on h2. } 38. Bb3 Re1 $19) 20... Rdg8 { Examine 21.d4 and 21.Bxf7. } 21. Raf1 (21. d4 { Black wins with either 21...exd4 or 21...Qxg2+. For example... } 21... Qxg2+ 22. Rxg2 Rxg2+ 23. Kh1 Bf3 24. Ng1 Rxg1+ { Double check as in the game. } 25. Kh2 R8g2+ $19 { etc. See the game. }) (21. Bxf7 Qxg2+ 22. Rxg2 Rxg2+ 23. Kh1 Bf3 24. Bxg8 Rxg8+ 25. Kh2 Rg2+ 26. Kh1 Rxf2+ 27. Kg1 Rxc2+ { A nice windmill tactic. }) 21... Qxg2+! 22. Rxg2 Rxg2+ 23. Kh1 Bf3 { What is Black's threat? Examine 24.Nf4.
-
A: ...Rxf2#. A deadly double check mate. } 24. Ng1 Rxg1+ 25. Kh2 Rxf1 { White resigned. Show that Black mates against any defense.
-
A: White can throw pieces away, but that is it. The threat is ...Rh1#. } 26. Qb1 Rgg1 27. Qxf1 Rxf1 28. Be6+ fxe6 29. b3 { Or anything else... } 29... Rh1# 0-1


