{ [TAM Test #2]
Play through the entire game before attempting to answer the questions. Summarize the game.
-
A: In a Torre Attack opening, both players make sub-optimal moves. Both players make several bad moves throughout the game. Nothing in either of those is unusual at every level of the game! Hence Silman's advice to hang tough tough because your opponent might make the next mistake.

"The blunders are all there on the board, waiting to be made." -Tartakower

"The winner is the one who makes the next-to-last mistake.” -Tartakower
- }
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bg5 { Research the Torre Attack in this position. } 3... d5 4. e3 { Silman says that White thought he was playing the Colle System, but with his Bishop on g5. Research the Colle System. } 4... Be7 5. Bd3 O-O { Stockfish has in order 6.h3, 6.Ne5, 6.0-0. Comment on this.
-
A:Stockfish is not presenting the moves in the master OB! Some chess programs will do that. So Stockfish is presenting its analysis of the variation tree - heavily weighted on the point count system. It cannot find an advantage for either player this way and so marks all the moves with a score of 0.
The bottom line is that you cannot get a description of themes or even positional characteristics from Stockfish. } 6. O-O { Silman: Natural but inaccurate. Better is 6.Nbd2 when 6...c5 ...
jomega: The OB on Lichess does not have a single Black win against 6.Nbd2. Finish Silman's comment by showing how White should meet 6.Nbd2 c5. Also describe what the plans should be for both sides.
-
A: See the next line for 6.Nbd2. White's plan should be to play on the kingside and for a kingside attack. Black should meet this with play in the center. Black should not lock the center! } (6. Nbd2 c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. Ne5 { ...with the threat of 9.f4. So since Silman says that Ne5 in the game was premature, why is he advocating that move here? Let's look at ...Nxe5. } 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Nd7 10. Bf4 { White wins the majority of games in the OB. (40%w,40%=,20%b). }) 6... c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. Nbd2 { Since White has now played the moves in Silman's line (with the addition of 0-0), why was Silman saying 6.0-0 was inaccurate?
-
A: Because 6.0-0 loses a tempo at a crucial moment for White's Ne5 move. Black can play 8...Qc7 and threaten 9...e5. } 8... b6 9. Qe2 { 9.Ne5 would have been good. } 9... cxd4? { What is wrong with this move? Comment on the move 9...c4.
-
A: It half-opens the e-file for White after 10.exd4 and gives Black few options for plans. 9...c4 would be terrible. It would lock the center. White would just retreat his attacked d3-Bishop. } 10. exd4 { You may have found in researching the Colle System that White plays ...c3 so as to recapture on d4 with the c-pawn. Did White's capture with e-pawn make sense?
-
A: Yes, it makes sense. White does not want to open the c-file here. This is *not* the Colle System. } 10... h6 11. Bh4 { Which of the 17 Soltis pawn structures do we have? What are the themes for both sides here?
-
A: This is the Queen's Gambit – Orthodox Exchange structure with colors reversed! See
https://lichess.org/study/B5upGe9A/HcMwXeCa
White should play for a kingside attack. Black can play for a minority attack or e3/e4 break. } 11... Bb7 12. Ne5? { Right idea at the wrong moment. Based on your answer to what the theme should be for White, suggest some details of a plan instead of this move 12.Ne5.
-
A: White can place his pieces as Rfe1, Rad1, Bb1, Qd3. Ne5 might later be indicated depending on the moves actually made by both sides. } 12... Nxe5 { Analyze 13.dxe5. } 13. Qxe5 (13. dxe5 Nd7 14. Bxe7 (14. Bg3!? Nc5 15. Bc2 { If White cannot play this, then his strategy is busted. } 15... Ba6 16. c4 { Black has the initiative with 16...dxc4 or 16...d4. }) 14... Qxe7 15. Bc2) 13... Nd7 14. Bxe7 { Forced. } 14... Nxe5 15. Bxd8 Nxd3 16. Be7 Rfe8 17. Ba3 b5? { Wanting to play ...a5 followed by ...b4, but what has Black overlooked?
-
A: His Knight is in danger. } 18. f4 { Examine 18...a5 with the idea of 19.Rf3 b4. Also examine Stockfish's idea of 18...b4. } 18... g5 (18... a5 19. Rf3 b4 20. Rxd3 bxa3 21. b3 $16 { White eventually wins the a3-pawn with his Knight. }) (18... b4! { Black gives up a pawn (temporarily) and gets the initiative. } 19. cxb4 Ba6 20. Rf3 Rab8 { Black's Knight escapes while regaining the pawn. }) 19. fxg5 hxg5 20. Rf6 { Examine Silman's suggestion of 20.g3 to take f4 away from Black's Knight. } (20. g3 { Silman is making the point that one needs to be aware of moves that restrict Knights. Black can survive here. For example... } 20... a5 21. Rf3 b4 22. Rxd3 bxa3 23. b3 { The idea from before, but before White had a pawn on f4. } 23... e5 { Black opens lines one way or the other. }) (20. Rf3 Nf4 { If Black's idea was to get his Knight out. } 21. Bd6 Ng6 22. Raf1 $16 { White gets his kingside attack. }) 20... Nf4? { What's a better move for Black? Analyze that. } (20... Kg7 21. Rf3 (21. Raf1? Nf4 $17) 21... b4 { An interesting idea. } 22. Rxd3 bxa3 23. b3 e5 { We saw this idea before. }) 21. Bd6 { Pick up White's f6-Rook and d6-Bishop and put them anywhere you want on the board! Where did you put them? Now find a defense for Black.
-
A: Ra8, Be5 checkmate! This was White's idea. Black can be bold and play ...Kg7. } 21... Nh5 (21... Kg7 22. Be5 Nh5 { White has a discovered check here, but it is hard to find a definitive win. } 23. Rf2+ (23. Rf5+ f6 24. Rxg5+ Kh6 25. Rxh5+ Kxh5 26. Bxf6 b4 27. Be5 bxc3 28. bxc3 Rac8 29. Rc1 { This looks awful for White. }) 23... f6 24. g4 fxe5 25. gxh5 exd4 26. cxd4 Rf8 27. Re1 Rxf2 28. Kxf2 Rf8+ 29. Kg3 Bc8 30. Nf3 Kh6 31. Ne5 Kxh5 32. Rc1 a5 33. Rc7 $16 { White's Knight is well placed versus Black's Bishop. White's Rook is active. }) 22. Rh6! Ng7 { Show that 22...Nf4 is no better. } (22... Nf4 23. Be5 Ng6 24. Bf6 $18) 23. Rf6 { What should White have played? } (23. Be5 $18 { Silman: ...meeting 23...Nf5 with the reasonably strong 24.Rh8#. }) 23... Nf5 24. Be5 { What is White's threat?
-
A: 24.Nf3 or 24.g4. } 24... g4 25. Rf1 b4 26. Nb3 { Examine 26.Rf4. } (26. Rf4 bxc3 27. bxc3 Kf8 28. Rxg4 Ke7 29. Nb3 $18) 26... bxc3 27. bxc3 Ba6 { Silman: White's unnecessary Knight move gave Black time to bring this Bishop into play.
jomega: White has a shot here. } 28. Rf4 (28. R1xf5 exf5 29. Rxa6 $18 { White has won two pieces for his Rook. }) 28... Be2 29. Kf2 Bd1 30. Nc5 { Do you think 30.Ke1 was better?
-
A: Yes, it is a clear win for White. } (30. Ke1 Bxb3 (30... Ne3 { The move White was afraid of. } 31. Rxf7 (31. Kd2 Nxg2 32. Kxd1 Nxf4 33. Bxf4 $18 { Silman: ... was hopeless for Black. }) 31... Nxg2+ 32. Kxd1 Nxf4 33. Rxf4 $19) 31. Rxg4+ Kf8 32. axb3 Ke7 33. Rf4 $18) 30... Rab8 31. Nd7 { Overlooking the obvious move and the not so obvious move. What were those?
-
A: Bxb8 or R4xf5. } (31. Bxb8 Rxb8 32. R4xf5 exf5 33. Rxf5 Rb2+ 34. Ke1 Bc2 35. Rxd5 Rxa2 36. Rg5+ Kf8 37. Rxg4 $18) (31. R4xf5 exf5 32. Bxb8 Rxb8 33. Rxf5 $18) 31... Rb2+ 32. Ke1 Ne3 { Threatening?
-
A: ...Re2#. } 33. Rf2 Rb1 { Is Black winning?
-
A: No. White can play Kd2 or Rh6. See the next variations. } 34. Rxf7?? (34. Kd2 Nc4+ 35. Kd3) (34. Rh6 Bf3+ 35. Kd2 Nc4+ 36. Kd3 Be4+ 37. Ke2 Rb2+ 38. Kd1 Rb1+ { Black has a draw. }) 34... Bf3+ 35. Kd2 Nc4+ { White resigned. Silman says "A sane look at the position would have convinced White that 35.Kd3 Kxf7 (35...Be4+ 36.Ke2) 36.gxf3 was worth playing out." What do you think of Silman's comment?
-
A: He is correct that White should play on. Black could make the next mistake! } 36. Kd3 (36. Kc2?? Be4#) 36... Rd1+ (36... Kxf7 37. gxf3 Rd1+ 38. Ke2 Rd2+ 39. Ke1 Rxf2 40. Kxf2 Re7 41. Nf6 Nxe5 42. dxe5 gxf3 $19) 37. Kc2 Ne3+ 38. Kb2 Kxf7 39. gxf3 Rg1 $19 0-1


