{ [HTWCGQ pg 85]
-
The opening turns into a Catalan. White plays some slightly inaccurate moves in the opening and Black's energetic play makes his pieces come alive. White over looks a tactic and soon loses his Queen and the game.
- }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 { This opening, where White avoids 3.Nc3 (the Nimzo-Indian), is called the Anti-Nimzo-Indian. } 3... d5 4. g3 { And this is the Catalan opening - a combination of the Queens Gambit and Reti openings. } 4... dxc4 5. Nbd2 { White decides to retrieve his pawn immediately, but this give Black time to strike the center. } (5. Bg2 a6 6. O-O Nc6 7. e3 Bd7 8. Qe2 b5) 5... c5 { White should have continued his plan of Nxc4. } 6. dxc5? (6. Nxc4 Nc6 7. Bg2 (7. dxc5 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 Ng4 $15) 7... cxd4 8. O-O Bc5 { White has to find some compensation for his lost pawn. }) 6... Bxc5 { Black is threatening ...Bxf2+. White should play 7.Qa4+ or 7.e3. } 7. Bg2?? { A developing move that misses the coming tactics. } 7... Bxf2+! 8. Kxf2? Ng4+ { White loses as we will see. } 9. Ke1 (9. Kg1 Qb6+ 10. Nd4 Qxd4+ 11. e3 Qxe3+ 12. Kf1 Qf2#) (9. Kf1 Ne3+ $19 { Winning the Queen. }) 9... Ne3 { White resigned.
White's Queen is trapped. } 10. Qa4+ Bd7 11. Qa3 (11. Qb4 Nc2+ $19) 11... Nc2+ $19 0-1


