{ [CMvCA #13]
-
Black plays an opening involving a double fianchetto. His attempt to have any say over the center does not occur until his 7th move - and that move is incorrect. White owns the center at that point. White conducts a kingside pawn attack and Black does not defend well. White wins.
- }
1. d4 b6 2. e4 { The classical center pawn formation. } 2... Bb7 3. Nd2 { An interesting choice.
Pros: Allows the c-pawn to move. The Knight cannot be pinned as it could if it was on c3.
Cons: Blocking the QB. Not influencing the center as much as a Knight on c3 would. } 3... g6 { Black intends to fianchetto the KB as well. } 4. Ngf3 Bg7 5. c3 { White now has a strong center. Depending on how Black plays, White might want his KB at e2, d3, or c2. } 5... d6 { Black tries to get some influence in the center by hitting at e5 and c5. } 6. Bc4 { The Bishop is certainly more active here or on d3 that it would be on e2. Note that 6.Bb5+ is pointless because after blocking the check with ...Nd7, Black could later play ...a3 and White's Bishop would have to retreat. } 6... e6 { Black has to be careful. On ...Nd7 White could try Bxf7+ followed by Ng5+. On ...Nf6 instead, White has e5 chasing the Knight while blocking up the Black KB's diagonal. Stockfish was looking at 6...e5, however that leaves White's QB hitting f7. White could continue his kingside action as in the game. } 7. h4 { The g6 pawn is a target. White has not castle and Black is in no position to attack White's King. } 7... d5? { An error in judgement of when to duck and when to punch. Black should have ducked with ...h5. } 8. Bd3 { Not 8.exd5 exd5 which only helps Black. } 8... Nf6 { Black should have played ...h5. Also ...c5 would be better than the text. } 9. e5 Ne4 { Black figures he has 2 defenders and White has two attackers; so his Knight is ok. } 10. Qc2 { But now White has 3 attackers on e4. The variation looks at what happens if Black tries to add a third defender with ...f5. } 10... Nxd2 (10... f5? 11. exf6 { You would not believe how many people do not know about en passant pawn captures. } 11... Nxf6 12. h5 Nxh5 13. Bxg6+ hxg6 14. Qxg6+ Kd7 15. Rxh5 Rxh5 16. Qxh5 $16) 11. Bxd2 { Can Black play ...h5 now? It does not work. See the next variation. } 11... Nd7 (11... h5? 12. Bxg6 fxg6 13. Qxg6+ Kf8 14. Ng5 Qe7 15. Nxe6+ Kg8 16. Bg5 Qf7 17. Qxg7+ Qxg7 18. Nxg7 Kxg7 19. Bf6+ Kh7 20. Bxh8 Kxh8 $18) 12. h5 c5 { Trying to counter attack in the center. See the variations for alternatives on the kingside. } (12... Nf8?? 13. h6 $18) (12... Qe7 13. h6 Bf8 14. Bg5 f6 15. exf6 Nxf6 16. Bxg6+ hxg6 17. Ne5! (17. Qxg6+ { etc. -CMvCA
jomega: This wins, but 17.Ne5 seems stronger. } 17... Qf7 18. Qxf6 Qxf6 19. Bxf6 Rxh6 20. Rxh6 Bxh6 21. g4) 17... O-O-O (17... Rg8 18. Ng4 O-O-O (18... Nxg4 19. Bxe7 $18) 19. Bxf6 Qf7 20. Bxd8 Kxd8 21. h7 Rh8 22. Ne5 Qf6 23. Qxg6 $18) 18. Qxg6 Qh7 19. Bxf6 Qxg6 20. Nxg6 Rxh6 21. Rxh6 Bxh6 22. Bxd8 Kxd8 $18) (12... O-O { Castling into an attack cannot be good. } 13. hxg6 hxg6 14. Bxg6 fxg6 15. Qxg6 Rxf3 { Desperation. } 16. Qh7+ Kf7 17. Qh5+ Kf8 18. Qxf3+ Kg8 19. Qg4 Qe7 { Trying to hold the e-pawn. } 20. O-O-O { Let's see what Stockfish has as a defense of this hopeless Black position. } 20... c5 21. Rh5 cxd4 22. Bh6 Re8 23. Bxg7 Qxg7 24. Rg5 Re7 25. Rxg7+ Rxg7 26. Qxe6+ { The e6-pawn falls. } 26... Rf7 27. Rxd4 Bc8 28. Rg4+ Kf8 29. Qd6+ { White's Queen and Rook dance around Black's pieces. } 29... Ke8 30. e6 Nf6 31. exf7+ Kxf7 32. Rf4 Be6 33. Qd8 { The pinned Knight falls. } 33... Kg7 34. Qxf6+ Kg8 35. Qg6+ Kh8 36. Rh4#) (12... gxh5 13. Bg5 Qc8 14. Bxh7 c5 15. O-O-O cxd4 16. Nxd4 Nxe5 17. Rhe1 Kd7 18. Bf5 exf5 19. Nxf5 Qf8 20. Nxg7 Qxg7 21. Qf5+ Kd6 22. Rxe5 { Ouch! } 22... Qxe5 23. Bf4 Qxf4+ 24. Qxf4+ Kc6 25. Qxf7 Rad8 26. Re1 Bc8 27. Re7 Bd7 { Now a small combination. } 28. Rxd7 Rxd7 29. Qf6+ Kb7 30. Qxh8 $18) 13. Bg5 Qc7 (13... f6 14. h6! { Better than exf6 given in CMvCA. } (14. exf6 Bxf6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. hxg6 hxg6 17. Rxh8+ Qxh8 18. O-O-O $18) (14. Bxg6+ Kf8 (14... hxg6?? 15. Qxg6+ $18) 15. exf6 Bxf6 16. Bh6+ Ke7 17. Bxh7 $18)) 14. hxg6 hxg6 (14... fxg6?? 15. Bxg6+ { Again better than 15.Rxh7 given in CMvCA. } (15. Rxh7 Rxh7 16. Bxg6+ Kf8 17. Bxh7 $18) 15... Kf8 (15... hxg6?? 16. Qxg6+ Kf8 17. Be7+ Kxe7 18. Qxg7+ Kd8 19. Rxh8+ Nf8 20. Rxf8#) 16. O-O-O h6 17. Bh4 Kg8 18. Rh3 Rf8 19. Be7 Rf4 20. Bd6 Qd8 21. Rdh1 Nf8 22. Rg3 Qd7 23. Bh5 Rf5 24. Kb1 Ba6 25. Bg4 Rf7 26. Nh4 h5 27. Bd1 cxd4 28. cxd4 Rf4 29. Qd2 Qf7 30. Bxf8 Qxf8 31. Ng6 Rxf2 32. Qe3 Qf5+ 33. Ka1 Qf7 34. Nxh8 Kxh8 35. Bf3 Rf1+ 36. Rxf1 Bxf1 37. Bxh5 Qxh5 38. Rh3 Qh7 39. Rxh7+ Kxh7 40. Qh3+ Bh6 41. Qxe6 Bc4 42. Qf5+ Kg7 43. Qg4+ Kf7 44. Qd7+ Kg6 45. e6 Bg5 46. e7 Bxe7 47. Qxe7 $18) 15. Rxh8+ Bxh8 16. Bxg6! cxd4?? { Better is ...Nf1. } 17. Bxf7+! Kxf7 18. Qh7+ Bg7 19. Bh6 Rg8 { The only move that holds the g7-Bishop. } 20. Ng5+ Ke7 { If instead ...Kf1 then Nxe6+ winning the Black Queen. } 21. Bxg7 { The threatened discovered check is deadly. Not 21.Qxg8 allowing 21...Qxe5+ 22.Kf1 Bxh6. } 21... Rxg7 22. Qxg7+ Ke8 (22... Kd8 23. Nxe6+ Ke8 24. Nxc7+ Kd8 25. Ne6+ Ke8 26. O-O-O $18 { and mate soon. }) 23. Qf7+ Kd8 24. Nxe6+ Kc8 25. Qe8+ Qd8 26. Qxd8# *


