[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 14"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/bZy1zHTh"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.19"]
[UTCTime "20:21:54"]

{ [CMvCA #20]
-
Black here is a strong amateur, but White is a master. Black's choice of opening (Queen Gambit Refused - Marshall) gives White the opportunity to break the rules-of-thumb to cramp Black's game. Black threatened to equalize after 9...Nc6 so White sacrificed a pawn. With just a Time lead and piece play, White hones in on a weak square in the Black camp (d6). White sacrifices again to remove the Bishop guardian of that square. White gets up in material with two minor pieces for a Rook and pawn while Black's King Rook is trapped in the corner. Black fatally weakens his f7 square. His second rank defense leaves the back rank vulnerable. White finds a combination to take advantage of the weaknesses and wins.
- }
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6?! { In the words of the Classical School - Black is about to give up the center. } 3. cxd5 { White has to break the rules-of-thumb by making another pawn move, however it is justified as we will see. } 3... Nxd5 4. Nf3 { Preventing ...e5 before striking at the Black Knight. } 4... e6 { This blocks the QB. } 5. e4 Nb6 6. Bd3 { CMvCA: Although it might seem natural to play 6.Nc3 first, since there is no doubt that this is the best square for the QN, White plays his KB to d3 at this point, because after 6.Nc3 c5 White cannot play 7.dxc5 without giving Black the opportunity to exchange Queens and thus lessen White's advantage.
jomega: Except that White in that line plays 7.Be3. Their point that White does not want to exchange Queens is well taken.
- } (6. Nc3 c5 7. Be3 (7. dxc5? Qxd1+ 8. Nxd1 Bxc5) 7... cxd4 8. Nxd4 Be7 (8... e5? { Trying to force the exchange of Queens but the tactics favor White. } 9. Ndb5 Qxd1+?? { A big mistake. } 10. Rxd1 Na6 11. Nxa7 Nd7 12. Bb5 { Black has no good reply to the threats. } 12... Nc7 13. O-O { Before attacking more, White castles. } 13... h5 14. Nd5 Nxb5 15. Nxb5 Ra5 16. Rc1 Kd8 17. a4 Rxa4? 18. Rfd1 Rxe4 19. Nb6 Ke7 20. Nxc8+ Kf6 21. Rxd7 $18)) 6... c5 { Attacking White's center. Note that Black is not threatening to win a pawn on d4 because his Queen would be left there unguarded after the exchanges and then Bb5+ would win the Queen with a discovered attack. The real issue is that White wants control of e5, hence 7.0-0 would be a mistake. } 7. dxc5 (7. O-O? cxd4 8. Nxd4 e5! (8... Qxd4?? 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Qxd4 $18)) 7... Bxc5 { White's advantage is pawn on 4th vs pawn on 3rd. We covered this in the Intermediate Course. See
https://lichess.org/study/ahGFC4eY } 8. O-O Qc7! { This is a good move by the amateur. Instead of ...0-0, he prepares for ...e5. } (8... e5 { In CMvCA they now give this move. } 9. Nxe5 Bxf2+?? { CMvCA says the master considers this. I hope the master would reject it because instead of the bad move 10.Kxf2 Qd4+, White just plays 10.Rxf2.
CMvCA: One of the big differences between strong amateur and the master is that the latter sees more, considers more. } 10. Rxf2 $18 { Black has nothing at all to show for his sacrifice!! }) 9. e5! { White pauses his "development" to cramp Black's game. } 9... Nc6 { Attacking e5 with 2 pieces. Does White have to defend with 10.Bf4? No! } 10. Nc3! Nxe5 (10... O-O 11. Bxh7+! Kxh7 12. Ng5+ $18 { White's attack wins. }) (10... Bd7 11. Bf4 Nb4 12. Ne4 Nxd3 13. Qxd3 Be7 14. Rac1 Qd8 $16) (10... Nd5 11. Nxd5 exd5 $16 { Black has an isolani. }) 11. Bf4 { Since 11...Bd6 fails to 12.Nb5 Black played ... } 11... Nxf3+ 12. Qxf3 { White is down a pawn but has 3 tempi (Black's Queen has to move), open lines, and piece play. } 12... Qe7 13. Ne4 { Played to control d6. } 13... Nd5 14. Rac1 { White wants the c5-Bishop, which guards d6, captured. } 14... b6 { Black figures the Bishop is now adequately guarded. } 15. Rxc5! Nxf4 (15... bxc5?? 16. Bd6 Qd7 17. Bxc5 Kd8 18. Qg3 f6 19. Nd6 { Threatening Bb5. } 19... g5 20. Bb5 Qc7 21. Rc1 a6 22. Ba4 Bd7 23. Bb3 Bc6 24. Qg4 Qd7 25. Bxd5 Bxd5 26. Qd4 Rf8 27. Bb6+ Ke7 28. Rc7 $18) 16. Rxc8+ Rxc8 17. Bb5+ Kf8 18. Qxf4 { White has two pieces for a Rook and pawn. } 18... e5? { Better was ...h6. Black has made a weakness of f7. } 19. Qf5 Rc7 20. f4 { Energetic play. } 20... exf4? { Better to give back the pawn. } (20... g6 21. Qxe5 Qxe5 22. fxe5 Kg7 $16 { White is better. }) 21. Qxf4 h5 { Black plans a Rook lift Rh6/Rf6 if White tries Nd6. The next White move is hard to see. } 22. Bc4!! { A decoy/deflection sacrifice. } 22... Kg8 { Black avoids ...Rxc4. } (22... Rxc4 23. Qb8+ Qe8 24. Qxe8+ Kxe8 25. Nd6+ Ke7 26. Nxc4 $18) (22... Rh6 23. Bxf7 Rf6 24. Nxf6 $18) (22... g5 23. Nxg5 Qc5+ 24. Rf2 Qxc4 25. Qxc7! Qxc7 26. Ne6+ Ke7 27. Nxc7 $18) 23. Nd6 Rh6 (23... Rxc4 24. Nxc4 $18) 24. Bxf7+ Kh8 25. Ne4 { Stopping ...Rf6. } 25... Rc8 { Hoping to pin the Bishop. } 26. Bd5 { Protecting the e4-Knight against ...Re8. } 26... Re8 27. h4 { Luft for the King and an outpost square at g5 for the Knight. } 27... Qe5 { There was no good move for Black. } 28. Qxe5 Rxe5 29. Rf8+ Kh7 30. Ng5+ Kg6 31. Be4+ { Black resigned. } 31... Rxe4 (31... Rf5 32. Bxf5#) 32. Nxe4 Kh7 33. Ng5+ Kg6 34. Kf2 $18 { This is comical! Black cannot get untied on the kingside. White's King will march over and grab the queenside pawns. } 1-0


