[UTCDate "2020.03.20"]
[UTCTime "14:21:22"]

{ [CMvCA #23]
-
The opening is the Alekhine-Chatard Attack in the French. Black plays well up to move 11, when he makes a strategic blunder that weakens his position and gives him no counterplay to White's kingside attack. White (the master) misses a tactically brilliant move on move 13, which Stockfish had no trouble finding instantly. The master plays masterfully after that missed opportunity. He presses the attack while hanging many of his pieces! It takes a forest of variations to prove the master's experience led him intuitively to the correct assessments, and White wins with a spectacular attack.
- }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 { White threatens e5 winning the pinned Knight. Black has a number of possible responses. The most popular move in the Lichess OB is the Burn Variation; 4...dxe4. Black's reply in this game of 4...Be7 use to be the main line. } 4... Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 { White could now play 6.Bxe7 leaving Black with a bad Bishop. } 6. h4 { Instead, White plays a gambit called the Alekhine-Chatard Attack. If Black accepts the pawn, White gets a half-open h-file for his Rook.
It is interesting to compare opening theory per the Lichess OB and CMvCA - currently a difference of almost 60 years!
The top 5 moves for Black here, per CMvCA, were c5, a6, h6, f6, and 0-0. In the OB they are Bxg5, h6, c5, a6, and Nc6. Theory changes, and so does fashion.
- } 6... f6 7. Qh5+ { The supposed refutation of 6...f6. } 7... Kf8 (7... g6 8. exf6! gxh5 9. fxe7 Qxe7 10. Bxe7 Kxe7 $16 { White is better because Black has weak h-pawns and a bad Bishop. White can win the h5-pawn immediately by attacking it with KN and KB. }) 8. exf6 { White wants to open lines to the Black King. } 8... Nxf6 (8... gxf6?? 9. Bh6+ Kg8 10. Qg4+ $18 { White will perform a Rook lift and Black is toast. }) 9. Qe2 { This strange move (it blocks the KB) is in accord with the position. Black's e-pawn is backward. White's Queen attacks it (and e5) and White will develop the KB at h3 where it will attack e6 as well. } 9... c5 { The amateur starts the right plan. Black must use his center majority to attack in the center. Ideal would be for Black to be able to play ...e5. } 10. Nf3 { White's plan is to restrict Black's attempt in the center, and then continue with his kingside attack. } 10... Nc6 11. O-O-O b6?? { A strategic blunder. Black's QB is needed to defend the e-pawn; so it would not be moving to b7. Black needed to continue to play in the center with ...cxd4. Also notice how this opens a diagonal leading to the undefended QR and weakens the QN and the light squares. } (11... cxd4 12. Nxd4 Nxd4 13. Rxd4 Bd7 (13... Qd6? { Suggested by CMvCA to hope for ...e5, but seems bad because of 14.Bf4. } 14. Bf4 $16) 14. h5 $16 { The idea is to play Rdh4. }) 12. g3 Kf7?? { Black wants to protect his weak e-pawn against the coming Bh3. This move is a tactical blunder per Stockfish! It is slightly complex and was not found by CMvCA. } 13. Bh3? { Continuing the attack on the e-pawn but missing the tactical shot. } (13. Ne5+!! { Black's QN is undefended so Black's next move is forced if he does not want to lose that Knight. Another consequence of the bad 11...b6 move. } 13... Nxe5 14. dxe5 { And Black has no defense against losing material! } 14... h6 (14... Ne8 15. Qf3+ Kg8 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Nxd5! exd5 18. Qxd5+ Be6 19. Qxa8 $18) (14... Nd7 15. Qf3+ Ke8 (15... Kg8 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Nxd5! Qf8 18. Ne7+ Qxe7 19. Qxa8 $18) 16. Nxd5 exd5 17. Qxd5 Rb8 18. e6 Nf6 (18... Bxg5+ 19. hxg5 $18) 19. Bb5+ Kf8 20. Qxd8+ Bxd8 21. Rxd8+ Ke7 (21... Ne8?? 22. Rxe8#) 22. Rxh8 $18) 15. exf6 hxg5 16. fxe7 Qxe7 17. Rd3 $18) 13... h6 14. Rhe1 { Ne5 was also still strong. } 14... hxg5? (14... Qd7 15. Ne5+! { Stronger than the suggestion in CMvCA of 15.Qb5. } (15. Qb5 Qe8 16. Qxc6 Qxc6 17. Ne5+ Kf8 18. Nxc6 $18) 15... Nxe5 16. dxe5 hxg5 17. exf6 Bxf6 18. Nxd5! Qc6 19. Nxf6 gxf6 20. Bg4 Rb8 21. hxg5 e5 22. Qc4+ Kg6 23. Bh5+! Rxh5 24. Qg8+ Kf5 25. gxf6 Kxf6 26. Qf8+ Kg6 27. Rd6+ Qxd6 28. Qxd6+ Kg7 29. Qxb8 $18) (14... Re8 { - Stockfish. But it loses to 15.Ne5+ and 15.Bxe6+; among others. }) 15. Bxe6+ Kf8 16. Nxg5 Nxd4 (16... cxd4 17. Nxd5 (17. Nf7 { This is more convincing. } 17... Qe8 18. Nxh8 dxc3 19. h5 cxb2+ 20. Kb1 g5 21. h6 Bxe6 22. Qxe6 Nb4 { Hoping to sac on a2 or c2, but... } 23. Ng6+ Qxg6 24. Qxe7+ Kg8 25. Qxb4 Rf8 26. Qd6 Qxh6 27. Rxd5 g4 28. Rf5 Rf7 29. Qe6 Qg7 30. Ree5 Kh8 31. Rg5 Qf8 32. Ref5 Qe7 33. Qxe7 Rxe7 34. Rxf6 $18) 17... Nxd5 18. Qf3+ Nf6 19. Qxc6 Rb8 20. Qc4 b5 21. Qb3 Qe8 22. Bf7 Qd8 23. Bg8 Qe8 24. Rxd4 { Threatening Rd8! } 24... Bf5 25. Bf7 Qc8 26. Rxe7! Qc5 (26... Kxe7 27. Qb4+ Qc5 28. Qxc5#) 27. Bg6 Qxe7 28. Bxf5 g6 29. Bxg6 Kg7 30. Bf5 Rb6 $18) 17. Rxd4 cxd4 18. Bxc8 Qe8 (18... Qxc8?? 19. Qxe7+ Kg8 20. Qf7#) (18... Rxc8?? 19. Ne6+ $18) 19. Nxd5 Bb4 (19... Nxd5 20. Qf3+ $18 { Losing the a8-Rook after the Knight interposes. }) (19... Rxc8 20. Nxe7 $18) (19... Kg8 20. Nxe7+ Kf8 21. Ng6+ Qxg6 22. Qe7+ Kg8 23. Be6+ Qf7 24. Qxf7#) 20. Ne6+ Kf7 21. Nxf6 { CMvCA now has two pages of variations to prove that White wins, yet says, "He [the master] does not analyze all the lines. He sees a few complications, then knows whether there will be a winning continuation. ... It is just a matter of intuition." Which is saying - experience. } 21... Qxc8 (21... gxf6 22. Qg4 Qg8 (22... Rg8 23. Qh5+ Rg6 (23... Ke7 24. Nc7+ { CMvCA: Discovered check winning the Queen. } 24... Bxe1 25. Nxe8 Rxe8 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Bg4 Bb4 28. c3 Bd6 29. Bh5 Re1+ 30. Kc2 Re2+ 31. Kb3 Re7 32. Qh8#) 24. Qh7+ Rg7 25. Qxg7#) 23. Ng5+ Kg7 (23... fxg5 24. Be6+ { CMvCA: Wins the Queen. }) (23... Kg6 24. Nf7+ Kxf7 (24... Kh7 25. Bf5+ Qg6 26. Qxg6#) 25. Be6+) 24. Be6 Bxe1 (24... Qf8 25. Nf7+ Kh7 26. Bf5#) (24... Qb8 25. Nh7+ Kxh7 (25... Kh6 26. Nxf6 Rg8 27. Qh5+ Kg7 28. Qf7+ Kh6 29. Qh7#) 26. Bf5+ Kh6 27. Qg6#) (24... Qe8 25. Nf7+ Kf8 26. c3 { CMvCA: White has two pawns for the exchange plus the attack... } (26. Nxh8 { This also wins. } 26... Bxe1 27. Ng6+ Kg7 28. Ne7+ Kh7 29. Nd5 $18)) 25. Bxg8 Raxg8 26. Nf3+ Kh6 27. Qf4+ Kg6 28. Nxe1 $18) (21... Kxf6 22. Qe5+ Ke7 (22... Kg6 23. Qxg7+ Kf5 24. Qg5#) (22... Kf7 23. Qxg7#) 23. Nxg7+ { CMvCA: Discovered check winning Queen. } (23. Qc7+ { jomega: Better is ... } 23... Qd7 24. Qxd7+ Kf6 25. Qxg7+ Kf5 26. Nxd4#)) 22. Ng5+ { Stockfish says mate in 9. So in this variation, the master's intuition was correct. } 22... Kxf6 (22... Kf8 23. Nd7+ Qxd7 (23... Kg8 24. Qe6#) 24. Qf3+ Kg8 25. Re7! Qxe7 26. Qxa8+ Qf8 27. Qd5+ Qf7 28. Qxf7#) 23. Qf3+ Kg6 (23... Qf5 24. Re6#) 24. Qf7+ Kh6 25. h5 { Black resigned.
Other than throwing pieces away we have... } 25... Qc6 26. Re6+ Qxe6 27. Nxe6 { There is no way to stop mate by Qg6. } 1-0


[Event "Advanced: Annotating Games (Unannotated): Annotate 16"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/Uw4P5qvl/3qgGS42Q"]
[Date "2020.03.20"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/jomega"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C15"]
[Opening "French Defense: Winawer Variation"]
[StudyName "Advanced: Annotating Games (Annotated)"]
[ChapterName "Annotate 16"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pEF9QCic/oOYrqEZF"]
[UTCDate "2020.03.20"]
[UTCTime "20:54:24"]

{ [CMvCA #23]
-
In a French Defense: Winawer Variation, White goes in for the complications of Qg4. When Black attacks an undefended Bishop, White self pins the Bishop. White fails to get the Bishop unpinned. Black destroys White's center and then continues the queenside attack by drawing White's King into a mating attack.
- }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 { The French Defense: Winawer Variation.
Black's plan is to double White's c-pawns and play against them. White will get the two Bishops and a kingside attack. } 4. a3 (4. e5 { The OB top move. } 4... c5 5. Bd2 { If White wants to avoid the doubled pawns. } (5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Qg4 { Black now has to decide whether to castle into an attack or sacrifice his g-pawn and then destroy White's center (...Qc7). }) 5... Ne7 6. Nb5 Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 O-O) 4... Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 dxe4 6. Qg4 Nf6 { Not surprisingly, the master goes in for complications. } 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 c5 { Immediately hitting at White's center. } 9. Ne2 { A good move, protecting both the d-pawn and c-pawn. } (9. Bb5+ { A check for checks sake, and it just leads to Black exchanging his bad Bishop. } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Nbxd7 $17) 9... Nc6 10. Bb2? { This is a really ugly move! } (10. dxc5 { Despite the tripled c-pawns, White has compensation in a pawn up and the Bishop pair. }) 10... Qb6 { Attacking the Bishop and d4 at the same time. } 11. Rb1? { A "self pin". The b8-Rook is unprotected and the b7-Bishop can make no useful discovery. } (11. Qxf6 Qxb2 { Black wins a pawn. }) (11. Qc1 { - CMvsCA }) (11. O-O-O { - Stockfish. }) 11... Rg6 12. Qh4? { Better was Qc1 unpinning the Bishop. } 12... cxd4 { To keep White from playing dxc5 and unpinning his Bishop after ...Qxc5. } 13. cxd4 Bd7 { The bad Bishop now has a good diagonal. Also the a8-Rook is now free to enter the game. } 14. Nf4 { This natural looking move loses material. } (14. Qf4 Rc8 15. Qc1 $17) 14... Nxd4 { Finishing the destruction of White's center. White has already lost. } 15. Nxg6?? { There was no time for this attempt to win the exchange. } (15. Bc4?? { CMvCA looks at this move for White and has 15.Nf5 for Black. But 15...Qa5+ would win quickly. So look at the line 15.Be2 as a replacement for their move. Their idea was that White wanted to castle to connect the Rooks, and this would allow White to get rid of the self-pin. } 15... Qa5+ 16. c3 Nf5 17. Qh3 Rh6 $19) (15. Be2 Nf5 16. Qh3 Rh6 17. Qc3 e3 18. O-O exf2+ 19. Rxf2 (19. Kh1 Ne4) 19... Qxf2+ 20. Kxf2 Ne4+ $19) 15... Nxc2+ { Stockfish says it is mate in 13. White's King gets pulled into a mating net. } 16. Kd1 Qd6+ 17. Kxc2 (17. Kc1 Rc8 18. Bc4 Rxc4 19. Qxf6 Nd4+ 20. Kd1 Nf3+ 21. Bd4 Rxd4+ 22. Qxd4 Qxd4+ 23. Kc2 Ba4+ 24. Rb3 Qc4+ 25. Kb1 Qxb3+ 26. Ka1 Qxa3+ 27. Kb1 Nd2#) 17... Ba4+ { Black's Bishop was bad, but active. } 18. Kc3 Rc8+ 19. Bc4 Qd3+ { The Bishop looks helplessly on. } 20. Kb4 Qxc4+ 21. Ka5 Qb5# 0-1


